WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.556 --> 00:00:07.522
- Hello everybody, welcome.

00:00:07.522 --> 00:00:09.395
I know people are still coming in.

00:00:09.395 --> 00:00:10.315
I'm Stephen Pitti.

00:00:10.315 --> 00:00:12.144
I'm professor of history,
American studies,

00:00:12.144 --> 00:00:14.274
and ethnicity, race, and
migration here at Yale,

00:00:14.274 --> 00:00:16.834
and I direct the Yale Center
for the Study of Race,

00:00:16.834 --> 00:00:19.192
Indigeneity, and Transnational Migration,

00:00:19.192 --> 00:00:20.992
I wanna thank my colleagues in RITM,

00:00:20.992 --> 00:00:23.601
Victoria, Mary, and Nicole,

00:00:23.601 --> 00:00:26.582
for helping to put this together today.

00:00:26.582 --> 00:00:28.652
Really excited to have all of you here.

00:00:28.652 --> 00:00:32.451
I learned a long time ago
that one wants to listen

00:00:32.451 --> 00:00:35.572
to everything that Laura Briggs says,

00:00:35.572 --> 00:00:39.002
and read everything that
Laura Briggs writes about

00:00:39.002 --> 00:00:43.203
many of the critical topics
facing the world right now,

00:00:43.203 --> 00:00:45.276
ranging from transnational
feminist movements

00:00:45.276 --> 00:00:47.493
to the politics of reproduction,

00:00:47.493 --> 00:00:51.063
intersections of empire and
migration, the welfare state,

00:00:51.063 --> 00:00:53.304
the health and safety of children,

00:00:53.304 --> 00:00:55.813
the past and future of
neo-liberal governance,

00:00:55.813 --> 00:00:58.215
and much, much more.

00:00:58.215 --> 00:01:01.283
She's just one of the most
thoughtful, and most persuasive,

00:01:01.283 --> 00:01:04.795
and most well-informed
people you'll ever meet.

00:01:04.795 --> 00:01:07.642
Professor Briggs is
professor of women's gender,

00:01:07.642 --> 00:01:08.655
and sexuality studies

00:01:08.655 --> 00:01:10.995
at the University of
Massachusetts at Amherst.

00:01:10.995 --> 00:01:12.635
She earned her PhD in American studies

00:01:12.635 --> 00:01:14.074
from Brown University.

00:01:14.074 --> 00:01:16.902
She's authored a number of field changing,

00:01:16.902 --> 00:01:19.704
and prize-winning books including:

00:01:19.704 --> 00:01:21.106
"Reproducing Empire:

00:01:21.106 --> 00:01:24.586
Race, Sex, and U.S.
Imperialism in Puerto Rico."

00:01:24.586 --> 00:01:26.585
"International Adoption:

00:01:26.585 --> 00:01:29.611
Global Inequalities and the
Circulation of Children."

00:01:29.611 --> 00:01:32.919
"How all Politics Became
Reproductive Politics:

00:01:32.919 --> 00:01:35.845
From Welfare Reform to
Foreclosure to Trump."

00:01:35.845 --> 00:01:38.864
And "Taking Children: A
History of American Terror."

00:01:38.864 --> 00:01:43.072
And she's co-edited
important volumes including:

00:01:43.072 --> 00:01:44.465
"Somebody's Children:

00:01:44.465 --> 00:01:47.794
The Politics of Transnational
and Transracial Adoption."

00:01:47.794 --> 00:01:50.064
And a special issue of "Meridians"

00:01:50.064 --> 00:01:52.505
on "Radical Transnationalism:

00:01:52.505 --> 00:01:56.482
Reimagining Solidarities,
Violence, Empires."

00:01:56.482 --> 00:01:58.553
She's now working on a book entitled

00:01:58.553 --> 00:02:01.984
"The Future is Born in Small Places:

00:02:01.984 --> 00:02:06.904
The Gendered Biopolitics of
Freedom, Debt, Imperialism,

00:02:06.904 --> 00:02:11.104
and a Natural Disaster in the Caribbean."

00:02:11.104 --> 00:02:14.105
We'll have to have you
back when that book's done.

00:02:14.105 --> 00:02:17.574
When Yale's RITM Center
considered who we'd like to invite

00:02:17.574 --> 00:02:20.072
to deliver our fall 2022 lecture,

00:02:20.072 --> 00:02:23.081
we knew right away that that
person was Laura Briggs.

00:02:23.081 --> 00:02:26.003
An outstanding scholar
and a great teacher.

00:02:26.003 --> 00:02:27.712
She's an active series editor

00:02:27.712 --> 00:02:29.825
at the University of California Press,

00:02:29.825 --> 00:02:33.433
a program builder who's worked
on the organizing collectives

00:02:33.433 --> 00:02:36.205
of the Tepoztlán Institute
for Transnational History,

00:02:36.205 --> 00:02:40.385
and the Thinking Transnational
Feminisms Summer Institute,

00:02:40.385 --> 00:02:43.003
and in the Migration Scholars Collective.

00:02:43.003 --> 00:02:45.723
And she's been an unceasing
and wise contributor

00:02:45.723 --> 00:02:48.973
to public conversations through
her appearances on radio,

00:02:48.973 --> 00:02:52.813
television and podcasts,
and in the print media.

00:02:52.813 --> 00:02:54.654
Her talk today is entitled,

00:02:54.654 --> 00:02:58.055
"Taking Children as a Tactic of Terror

00:02:58.055 --> 00:02:59.843
Producing Neoliberalism,

00:02:59.843 --> 00:03:03.082
and Making a Crisis of
Immigration and Asylum."

00:03:03.082 --> 00:03:04.894
Thank you so much for
coming, Laura Briggs.

00:03:04.894 --> 00:03:08.061
(audience applauding)

00:03:12.074 --> 00:03:14.864
- Thank you Stephen for
that lovely introduction.

00:03:14.864 --> 00:03:17.255
I have to tell you all, as
I was driving down here,

00:03:17.255 --> 00:03:20.213
I was listening to an audio book.

00:03:20.213 --> 00:03:24.256
I was listening to Celeste
Ng's "Our Missing Hearts,"

00:03:24.256 --> 00:03:26.204
which is about lost children,

00:03:26.204 --> 00:03:28.972
and it's about the fight

00:03:28.972 --> 00:03:33.181
to even be able to tell the
stories of lost children,

00:03:33.181 --> 00:03:36.764
so it was quite a
fitting thing to be doing

00:03:38.199 --> 00:03:41.090
as I was trying to get
ready for this talk,

00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:43.850
so thank you so much for
giving me this opportunity

00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:45.183
to do just that.

00:03:47.690 --> 00:03:50.507
So in April and May of 2018,

00:03:50.507 --> 00:03:52.390
the Trump administration's policy

00:03:52.390 --> 00:03:54.510
of separating children from parents

00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:56.502
mostly from Central America

00:03:56.502 --> 00:04:01.263
seeking asylum in the United
States made headlines.

00:04:01.263 --> 00:04:03.991
Photos of children torn
from their parent's arms

00:04:03.991 --> 00:04:06.583
filled the media in the United States,

00:04:06.583 --> 00:04:10.661
and audio tape of separated
children in a shelter crying

00:04:10.661 --> 00:04:12.533
seemed to be everywhere.

00:04:12.533 --> 00:04:14.531
Calling the policy Zero Tolerance,

00:04:14.531 --> 00:04:17.315
then U.S. Attorney General, Jeff Sessions,

00:04:17.315 --> 00:04:20.683
and Homeland Security
Chief, Kirstjen Nielsen,

00:04:20.683 --> 00:04:24.045
said "They were trying to deter people

00:04:24.045 --> 00:04:27.054
from petitioning for asylum."

00:04:27.054 --> 00:04:29.005
In the face of massive protests,

00:04:29.005 --> 00:04:32.375
and a court order to reunite
parents and children,

00:04:32.375 --> 00:04:35.054
the Trump administration
walked the policy back,

00:04:35.054 --> 00:04:38.235
and announced they would no longer do it.

00:04:38.235 --> 00:04:39.695
Nine months later, though,

00:04:39.695 --> 00:04:43.245
children dying in customs and
border protection custody,

00:04:43.245 --> 00:04:47.303
and a flu epidemic in a
detention camp in Texas

00:04:47.303 --> 00:04:49.314
brought doctors and lawyers,

00:04:49.314 --> 00:04:53.575
then lawmakers and activists to the camps.

00:04:53.575 --> 00:04:54.995
Border patrol was, in fact,

00:04:54.995 --> 00:04:57.177
still separating children from parents

00:04:57.177 --> 00:05:00.632
more than 5,000 of them in total.

00:05:00.632 --> 00:05:03.685
They were holding them for
weeks and months without beds,

00:05:03.685 --> 00:05:06.173
blankets, showers, or change of clothes

00:05:06.173 --> 00:05:09.356
in processing stations
designed to hold adults

00:05:09.356 --> 00:05:11.273
for less than 48 hours.

00:05:13.686 --> 00:05:17.269
One doctor described
the squalid conditions

00:05:18.188 --> 00:05:20.208
where children were being held

00:05:20.208 --> 00:05:24.629
as tantamount to the
intentional spread of disease,

00:05:24.629 --> 00:05:27.237
calling them torture facilities.

00:05:27.237 --> 00:05:29.479
Until the Trump administration figured out

00:05:29.479 --> 00:05:32.761
how to use the pandemic
and public health laws

00:05:32.761 --> 00:05:34.779
to exclude asylum seekers,

00:05:34.779 --> 00:05:37.296
customs and border protection

00:05:37.296 --> 00:05:40.520
deliberately targeted parents and children

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:43.179
separating them at the
border as part of a policy

00:05:43.179 --> 00:05:47.378
meant to deter asylum seekers
from entering the country.

00:05:47.378 --> 00:05:49.727
The cruelty was the point,

00:05:49.727 --> 00:05:52.830
as Adam Serwer at "The
Atlantic" first said.

00:05:52.830 --> 00:05:55.628
A widely circulated photo of a father,

00:05:55.628 --> 00:05:58.716
and his toddler daughter
floating dead in the Rio Grande

00:05:58.716 --> 00:06:01.726
suggested the terror
that asylum seekers faced

00:06:01.726 --> 00:06:05.276
while being metered in
Mexico for weeks and months,

00:06:05.276 --> 00:06:08.257
unable to cross the border
and petition for asylum,

00:06:08.257 --> 00:06:11.183
and then forcing them into refugee camps

00:06:11.183 --> 00:06:15.235
on the U.S. Mexico border like this one.

00:06:15.235 --> 00:06:17.025
There's a great deal to criticize

00:06:17.025 --> 00:06:21.384
in the Biden administration's
response to the asylum crisis,

00:06:21.384 --> 00:06:24.005
especially from Latin America.

00:06:24.005 --> 00:06:26.466
With a record low number of immigrants,

00:06:26.466 --> 00:06:28.966
and refugees admitted to the U.S.

00:06:28.966 --> 00:06:32.107
excepting the two years of the pandemic.

00:06:32.107 --> 00:06:34.638
And what I want you to see on this slide

00:06:34.638 --> 00:06:39.388
is that the ceiling for refugees
has been set quite high,

00:06:40.846 --> 00:06:44.315
but the actual number of refugees admitted

00:06:44.315 --> 00:06:47.565
is way down in that yellow orange line.

00:06:49.856 --> 00:06:50.855
Until this month,

00:06:50.855 --> 00:06:55.332
the Biden administration had
begun to empty refugee camps

00:06:55.332 --> 00:06:58.366
of mostly Central American
migrants in Mexico,

00:06:58.366 --> 00:07:02.075
like the one from the
previous slide in Matamoros,

00:07:02.075 --> 00:07:06.935
camps that at their height
contain thousands of people.

00:07:06.935 --> 00:07:09.686
This administration has actually worked

00:07:09.686 --> 00:07:12.145
to reunite children and families,

00:07:12.145 --> 00:07:17.035
successfully putting back
together around 500 of them

00:07:17.035 --> 00:07:19.746
that were separated by
the Trump administration,

00:07:19.746 --> 00:07:23.906
although, 200 still
remain lost to each other.

00:07:23.906 --> 00:07:28.104
In recent weeks as a new
wave of Venezuelans flee,

00:07:28.104 --> 00:07:30.627
economic collapse at home,

00:07:30.627 --> 00:07:35.423
shelters and encampments in
Mexico have begun to fill again,

00:07:35.423 --> 00:07:39.285
even more seemingly under
pressure from Washington,

00:07:39.285 --> 00:07:42.495
rumor has had it that Mexican officials

00:07:42.495 --> 00:07:45.525
are pushing Venezuelans
still further south

00:07:45.525 --> 00:07:48.654
across the border into Guatemala.

00:07:48.654 --> 00:07:52.255
And even as the Biden administration
is engaged in lawsuits

00:07:52.255 --> 00:07:55.005
to end expulsions under Title 42,

00:07:56.293 --> 00:07:59.423
the public health measure that's
been used to deport people

00:07:59.423 --> 00:08:01.744
since the COVID pandemic began

00:08:01.744 --> 00:08:05.477
without any semblance
of an asylum process,

00:08:05.477 --> 00:08:08.397
it has recently expanded
such expulsions again

00:08:08.397 --> 00:08:11.723
by using them against Venezuelans,

00:08:11.723 --> 00:08:15.056
just as it did months ago to Ukrainians.

00:08:17.242 --> 00:08:18.101
Above all, of course,

00:08:18.101 --> 00:08:20.603
what people need from the
U.S. immigration system

00:08:20.603 --> 00:08:22.002
is a simple, efficient,

00:08:22.002 --> 00:08:24.980
and predictable pathway to citizenship,

00:08:24.980 --> 00:08:27.442
not the Byzantine arbitrary,

00:08:27.442 --> 00:08:30.071
and capricious system we have now.

00:08:30.071 --> 00:08:32.222
Children and adults are crossing borders

00:08:32.222 --> 00:08:34.442
for any number of reasons,

00:08:34.442 --> 00:08:38.491
but these include importantly
seeking to reunite

00:08:38.491 --> 00:08:41.420
after many decades of a
racialized labor system

00:08:41.420 --> 00:08:44.691
in the United States that
wants immigrant workers,

00:08:44.691 --> 00:08:48.941
but not their households,
not children, not elders.

00:08:50.952 --> 00:08:53.370
The ongoing disruptions of everyday life

00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:55.463
for impoverished people
in Central America,

00:08:55.463 --> 00:08:57.500
Venezuela and Mexico

00:08:57.500 --> 00:09:00.612
has produced an asylum crisis,

00:09:00.612 --> 00:09:04.272
and this includes the ways
that the IMF, World Bank,

00:09:04.272 --> 00:09:08.150
and NGOs have methodically
destroyed national governments

00:09:08.150 --> 00:09:10.991
in many nations in Central America,

00:09:10.991 --> 00:09:13.350
opening the door to corruption,

00:09:13.350 --> 00:09:16.892
and to drug cartels taking
over municipal governments,

00:09:16.892 --> 00:09:20.343
and incorporating themselves as NGOs,

00:09:20.343 --> 00:09:23.329
building organizations
that extend from Honduras

00:09:23.329 --> 00:09:25.852
to the U.S. Southwest

00:09:25.852 --> 00:09:29.300
made much worse by state sponsored
violence in the aftermath

00:09:29.300 --> 00:09:32.826
of the U.S. sponsored
coup in Honduras in 2009,

00:09:32.826 --> 00:09:35.493
and contested elections in 2017.

00:09:37.732 --> 00:09:42.591
The ways opponents of the child
separation policy organized

00:09:42.591 --> 00:09:44.221
both helped and hindered the effort

00:09:44.221 --> 00:09:46.388
to address these problems.

00:09:47.560 --> 00:09:49.360
The first thing we have to acknowledge

00:09:49.360 --> 00:09:52.639
is that there's nothing
exceptional about taking children

00:09:52.639 --> 00:09:55.139
from their kin and caregivers.

00:09:56.401 --> 00:09:58.891
This has been a practice
up and down the Americas

00:09:58.891 --> 00:10:00.981
for four centuries,

00:10:00.981 --> 00:10:02.610
targeting single mothers,

00:10:02.610 --> 00:10:06.142
and others without
heteronormative families,

00:10:06.142 --> 00:10:10.892
especially enslaved people
and members of tribal nations.

00:10:12.832 --> 00:10:15.231
Treating child separation as remarkable,

00:10:15.231 --> 00:10:18.234
in some ways kept us from
actually stopping it,

00:10:18.234 --> 00:10:22.933
as it allowed the Trump
administration to claim in 2019

00:10:22.933 --> 00:10:25.392
that it was just taking
children from parents

00:10:25.392 --> 00:10:27.142
who had been neglectful,

00:10:27.142 --> 00:10:30.041
or who had a criminal history.

00:10:30.041 --> 00:10:31.762
We've not been good ever

00:10:31.762 --> 00:10:33.812
at addressing the ways these charges

00:10:33.812 --> 00:10:37.080
have a history in this country
of being directed at Black,

00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:40.153
and Indigenous communities in rebellion,

00:10:40.153 --> 00:10:43.233
and all the people Cathy
Cohen identified as queer,

00:10:43.233 --> 00:10:44.890
the welfare mothers,

00:10:44.890 --> 00:10:47.482
the single moms, the unruly youth,

00:10:47.482 --> 00:10:51.315
and all those who are
bad at nuclear families.

00:10:55.782 --> 00:10:58.461
In order to understand
the Trump era policy

00:10:58.461 --> 00:11:00.587
of taking children from asylum seekers,

00:11:00.587 --> 00:11:03.050
and putting them in detention camps,

00:11:03.050 --> 00:11:05.230
we need to ask how Central Americans

00:11:05.230 --> 00:11:07.061
were made into the sorts of people

00:11:07.061 --> 00:11:10.701
who could be dispossessed
of their children.

00:11:10.701 --> 00:11:14.097
How did it come to be
in the spring of 2018

00:11:14.097 --> 00:11:17.589
that immigration officials
walked the bridge of the Americas

00:11:17.589 --> 00:11:19.877
between Juárez in Mexico,

00:11:19.877 --> 00:11:23.109
and picked Central Americans
out of crowds of people

00:11:23.109 --> 00:11:25.010
from every country, continent,

00:11:25.010 --> 00:11:28.180
and island that walked that sky bridge?

00:11:28.180 --> 00:11:31.454
Why were Central Americans
in particular told

00:11:31.454 --> 00:11:35.549
that the United States was
full and they could not cross?

00:11:35.549 --> 00:11:37.421
As one immigration activist said,

00:11:37.421 --> 00:11:41.131
"If you look Indigenous or
you look Central American,

00:11:41.131 --> 00:11:42.669
they will stop you.

00:11:42.669 --> 00:11:44.998
They never ask why you are coming.

00:11:44.998 --> 00:11:48.329
They just say they can't receive you."

00:11:48.329 --> 00:11:50.519
The policy didn't spring fully formed

00:11:50.519 --> 00:11:54.301
from the head of a Trump
administration official in 2018.

00:11:54.301 --> 00:11:57.528
It had a history in the
anti-communist civil wars

00:11:57.528 --> 00:11:59.771
of the latter part of the 20th century

00:11:59.771 --> 00:12:02.889
when Central Americans first
began losing their children

00:12:02.889 --> 00:12:04.222
to state terror.

00:12:08.330 --> 00:12:11.981
Latin Americans in the
1960s, '70s, and '80s

00:12:11.981 --> 00:12:13.996
saw child taking by those

00:12:13.996 --> 00:12:17.836
who sought to instantiate
neoliberalism across the region

00:12:17.836 --> 00:12:20.839
through anti-communist rhetoric.

00:12:20.839 --> 00:12:23.821
There in the space
between Henry Kissinger,

00:12:23.821 --> 00:12:27.060
and Ronald Reagan's
America, fascist Spain,

00:12:27.060 --> 00:12:30.909
and Latin America's own
autonomous nationalism,

00:12:30.909 --> 00:12:34.410
militarism, and right-wing Catholicism,

00:12:34.410 --> 00:12:37.358
increasingly paranoid
anti-communist states

00:12:37.358 --> 00:12:39.968
sought to rigidly control
not only the present,

00:12:39.968 --> 00:12:41.218
but the future.

00:12:42.380 --> 00:12:45.147
Military dictatorships
up and down Latin America

00:12:45.147 --> 00:12:48.234
from Guatemala to Argentina,

00:12:48.234 --> 00:12:51.488
directed campaigns of
torture, assassination,

00:12:51.488 --> 00:12:53.508
and disappearance.

00:12:53.508 --> 00:12:56.649
While initially these right-wing
military dictatorships

00:12:56.649 --> 00:12:59.727
targeted armed leftist guerrillas,

00:12:59.727 --> 00:13:03.207
their victims grew more diverse
as they quickly overwhelmed,

00:13:03.207 --> 00:13:05.703
and largely killed actual militants,

00:13:05.703 --> 00:13:07.175
turning to those

00:13:07.175 --> 00:13:10.446
who supported anything
even vaguely socialist

00:13:10.446 --> 00:13:14.529
from unions to students
to agrarian cooperatives.

00:13:15.836 --> 00:13:17.546
Then they took their children

00:13:17.546 --> 00:13:19.856
hoping to ensure that the very young

00:13:19.856 --> 00:13:22.889
who might have been touched
by their parents leftist ideas

00:13:22.889 --> 00:13:26.186
were violently uprooted and reeducated

00:13:26.186 --> 00:13:28.369
in conservative adoptive families,

00:13:28.369 --> 00:13:30.286
or outside the country.

00:13:33.187 --> 00:13:36.807
This right-wing dream of killing ideas

00:13:36.807 --> 00:13:39.007
by burying their proponents,

00:13:39.007 --> 00:13:42.025
and taking their children was a failure.

00:13:42.025 --> 00:13:43.538
As the extent of torture,

00:13:43.538 --> 00:13:45.597
and especially the kidnapping of children

00:13:45.597 --> 00:13:47.999
became known to ever wider circles,

00:13:47.999 --> 00:13:51.582
there were first efforts
to prosecute them.

00:13:52.417 --> 00:13:54.834
In 2006, Jorge Rafael Videla,

00:13:55.978 --> 00:13:58.584
once the most powerful man in Argentina

00:13:58.584 --> 00:14:00.767
as its military dictator,

00:14:00.767 --> 00:14:04.486
went to prison together with
a number of his collaborators,

00:14:04.486 --> 00:14:06.936
not for his responsibility
for torture, murder,

00:14:06.936 --> 00:14:08.987
and disappearances,

00:14:08.987 --> 00:14:10.746
but rather for kidnapping children,

00:14:10.746 --> 00:14:13.518
and running an illegal adoption ring,

00:14:13.518 --> 00:14:15.459
while he was the defacto president

00:14:15.459 --> 00:14:18.126
in the late '70s and early '80s.

00:14:19.299 --> 00:14:20.780
While the Argentine Congress

00:14:20.780 --> 00:14:24.840
had tried to grant a general
amnesty to the former leaders

00:14:24.840 --> 00:14:28.760
to prevent their prosecution
for the crimes they committed,

00:14:28.760 --> 00:14:31.751
as they oversaw hundreds
of torture centers,

00:14:31.751 --> 00:14:34.871
and while giving orders for
the military to kill civilians,

00:14:34.871 --> 00:14:37.819
a judge found that the official pardon

00:14:37.819 --> 00:14:39.901
didn't cover child kidnapping

00:14:39.901 --> 00:14:42.810
because it was an ongoing crime

00:14:42.810 --> 00:14:47.340
continuing after the
final date of the amnesty.

00:14:47.340 --> 00:14:50.549
Kidnapped infants and children
turned out to be the crime

00:14:50.549 --> 00:14:52.786
they could be prosecuted for,

00:14:52.786 --> 00:14:54.780
and it's what held them in prison

00:14:54.780 --> 00:14:58.697
until the amnesty was
finally repealed in 2005.

00:15:02.019 --> 00:15:04.999
In El Salvador in the late 1970s,

00:15:04.999 --> 00:15:08.920
more and more people despaired
of free and fair elections

00:15:08.920 --> 00:15:12.302
as demonstrating for
them got people killed,

00:15:12.302 --> 00:15:17.042
and they turned to support the
leftist military guerrillas.

00:15:17.042 --> 00:15:20.453
In the U.S. the Carter
administration to a small degree,

00:15:20.453 --> 00:15:23.132
and then the Reagan
administration massively

00:15:23.132 --> 00:15:25.774
gave a corrupt Salvadoran state support

00:15:25.774 --> 00:15:28.894
in the form of training for the military.

00:15:28.894 --> 00:15:31.442
They gave them weapons, military advisors,

00:15:31.442 --> 00:15:36.442
and ultimately about one to
two million U.S. dollars a day

00:15:36.582 --> 00:15:40.220
to prop up a brutal and
unpopular government.

00:15:40.220 --> 00:15:43.174
The Salvadoran government
held power through terror,

00:15:43.174 --> 00:15:46.031
detentions and disappearances,

00:15:46.031 --> 00:15:48.613
death squads that left
bodies in the streets

00:15:48.613 --> 00:15:51.951
as a warning to others about
opposing the government,

00:15:51.951 --> 00:15:54.113
and massacres of civilian communities

00:15:54.113 --> 00:15:58.051
that were suspected of
support for the guerrillas.

00:15:58.051 --> 00:16:00.292
During the armed internal conflict,

00:16:00.292 --> 00:16:03.663
an estimated 75,000 civilians were killed

00:16:03.663 --> 00:16:06.783
in a country of only five million people.

00:16:06.783 --> 00:16:11.611
The overwhelming majority died
at the hands of the military.

00:16:11.611 --> 00:16:14.224
Military and paramilitary
forces in El Salvador

00:16:14.224 --> 00:16:18.382
also took between 800 and 1,000 children.

00:16:18.382 --> 00:16:21.552
During the conflict, the
military sought to eliminate

00:16:21.552 --> 00:16:23.991
the social base of the guerrilla.

00:16:23.991 --> 00:16:27.522
Drawing on the U.S. military's
paradigm in Vietnam,

00:16:27.522 --> 00:16:31.284
they sought to eliminate
the water, the fish,

00:16:31.284 --> 00:16:33.660
of the guerrillas swam in,

00:16:33.660 --> 00:16:36.871
engaging in a scourged earth
campaign against civilians,

00:16:36.871 --> 00:16:40.801
and creating model villages
surrounded by barbed wire,

00:16:40.801 --> 00:16:43.849
and guarded by military
and paramilitary units

00:16:43.849 --> 00:16:46.766
in place of autonomous communities.

00:16:47.678 --> 00:16:52.179
Their goals included breaking
people's will to resist

00:16:52.179 --> 00:16:56.280
by sowing terror including
of losing children,

00:16:56.280 --> 00:16:59.131
and as in Argentina to control the future

00:16:59.131 --> 00:17:03.249
by refusing to allow those
suspected of being communists

00:17:03.249 --> 00:17:05.832
from raising a next generation.

00:17:06.900 --> 00:17:09.739
Sometimes children became
lost in the confusion

00:17:09.739 --> 00:17:13.640
when military threat
forced communities to flee.

00:17:13.640 --> 00:17:16.380
Others were picked up right
in front of their parents,

00:17:16.380 --> 00:17:18.003
and there were cases

00:17:18.003 --> 00:17:21.841
where they were literally
ripped from their mother's arms.

00:17:21.841 --> 00:17:25.459
According to their
reconstruction and research

00:17:25.459 --> 00:17:29.430
from the stories of surviving relatives,

00:17:29.430 --> 00:17:30.281
human rights groups

00:17:30.281 --> 00:17:33.241
attributed 90% of the
child disappearance cases

00:17:33.241 --> 00:17:34.841
to the military,

00:17:34.841 --> 00:17:37.424
and 10% to the guerrillas.

00:17:38.512 --> 00:17:41.931
In late May and early June of 1982,

00:17:41.931 --> 00:17:43.868
to take only one example,

00:17:43.868 --> 00:17:47.562
in the events that became
known as the Guinda de Mayo,

00:17:47.562 --> 00:17:49.672
the Flight in May,

00:17:49.672 --> 00:17:52.183
people ran from their homes
because they were tipped off

00:17:52.183 --> 00:17:54.523
that paramilitary groups in the army

00:17:54.523 --> 00:17:57.283
were planning to come to
their homes and kill them,

00:17:57.283 --> 00:18:00.833
believing that they were
supporters of the guerrillas.

00:18:00.833 --> 00:18:04.243
The military called
this Operation Clean-Up,

00:18:04.243 --> 00:18:06.331
Operación Limpieza,

00:18:06.331 --> 00:18:11.331
designed to wipe out communities
in Northeast Chalatenango.

00:18:11.921 --> 00:18:14.503
People fled from one community to another

00:18:14.503 --> 00:18:18.294
with soldiers on their heels
who killed anyone they found.

00:18:18.294 --> 00:18:22.982
They slaughtered animals and
destroyed homes and crops.

00:18:22.982 --> 00:18:26.192
These forces also took
at least 11 children,

00:18:26.192 --> 00:18:29.940
including Ernestina and
Erlinda Serrano Cruz,

00:18:29.940 --> 00:18:32.162
ages three and seven.

00:18:32.162 --> 00:18:35.453
When the Serrano Cruz
family began running,

00:18:35.453 --> 00:18:39.343
the mother and a brother made
it past military barricade,

00:18:39.343 --> 00:18:41.154
and went in one direction,

00:18:41.154 --> 00:18:43.845
while the father, brother and sisters,

00:18:43.845 --> 00:18:48.013
Suyapa, Ernestina and
Erlinda went in another.

00:18:48.013 --> 00:18:51.983
After three days of walking,
they made it to a settlement.

00:18:51.983 --> 00:18:55.823
They hid there for another three
days without food or water,

00:18:55.823 --> 00:18:59.565
but finally the brothers
went to search for water.

00:18:59.565 --> 00:19:03.865
Ernestina and Erlinda, small
children alone began to cry,

00:19:03.865 --> 00:19:07.448
tipping off soldiers
to the their presence.

00:19:09.080 --> 00:19:10.917
Suyapa was not far away,

00:19:10.917 --> 00:19:13.047
but she had a six-month-old baby,

00:19:13.047 --> 00:19:14.879
and she had tried to protect her family

00:19:14.879 --> 00:19:18.588
by hiding separately
in case the baby cried.

00:19:18.588 --> 00:19:21.307
She later reported that
she heard one soldier ask

00:19:21.307 --> 00:19:26.046
if they should take Ernestina
and Erlinda, or kill them.

00:19:26.046 --> 00:19:28.677
Another replied that
they should take them.

00:19:28.677 --> 00:19:30.886
When she no longer heard the soldiers,

00:19:30.886 --> 00:19:33.707
Suyapa went and searched for her sisters,

00:19:33.707 --> 00:19:35.399
but they were gone.

00:19:35.399 --> 00:19:38.479
The little girls were never found.

00:19:38.479 --> 00:19:41.518
After the war, a human rights group,

00:19:41.518 --> 00:19:46.518
la Asociación Pro-Búsqueda de
Niñas y Niños Desaparecidos,

00:19:46.850 --> 00:19:50.960
the Association For Searching
For Disappeared Children

00:19:50.960 --> 00:19:53.111
began the painful work with families

00:19:53.111 --> 00:19:57.438
of trying to find children
disappeared during the war.

00:19:57.438 --> 00:20:01.090
They found about 500, some
of them adopted in Europe,

00:20:01.090 --> 00:20:05.178
some in the United States,
others in Salvador,

00:20:05.178 --> 00:20:07.928
and continued to look for others.

00:20:09.438 --> 00:20:12.397
While it took decades of
work for their lawyers,

00:20:12.397 --> 00:20:14.970
the case that ultimately
broke open the impunity

00:20:14.970 --> 00:20:17.991
surrounding the military child taking

00:20:17.991 --> 00:20:21.430
was that of the Serrano Cruz girls.

00:20:21.430 --> 00:20:25.180
The Inter-American Court of
Human Rights ruled in 2005

00:20:25.180 --> 00:20:29.020
that the state was responsible
for their disappearances,

00:20:29.020 --> 00:20:32.648
and demanded that it opened
its records to help find them,

00:20:32.648 --> 00:20:36.207
and form a national search
commission, which it did,

00:20:36.207 --> 00:20:38.957
albeit not until six years later.

00:20:40.167 --> 00:20:42.738
The court also found
that the military unit

00:20:42.738 --> 00:20:47.167
that took the girls was the
infamous Atlacatl Battalion,

00:20:47.167 --> 00:20:50.139
responsible for numerous massacres,

00:20:50.139 --> 00:20:52.708
and atrocities during the war.

00:20:52.708 --> 00:20:54.409
This is particularly significant

00:20:54.409 --> 00:20:58.697
for the still unanswered question
of whether U.S. military,

00:20:58.697 --> 00:21:01.488
and intelligence advisors

00:21:01.488 --> 00:21:05.047
widely believed to have
trained Salvadorans in torture,

00:21:05.047 --> 00:21:08.068
and responsible for their
scorched earth strategy,

00:21:08.068 --> 00:21:12.657
were also involved with Salvador's
child kidnapping policy.

00:21:12.657 --> 00:21:16.361
The Atlacatl Battalion was
trained by the U.S. military

00:21:16.361 --> 00:21:19.327
in the School of the
Americas in North Carolina,

00:21:19.327 --> 00:21:21.449
and was directly advised by them

00:21:21.449 --> 00:21:24.361
once they returned to El Salvador.

00:21:24.361 --> 00:21:26.349
"The New York Times" called the battalion

00:21:26.349 --> 00:21:28.967
the pride of the United
States military team

00:21:28.967 --> 00:21:30.300
in San Salvador.

00:21:31.298 --> 00:21:35.276
Even if U.S. advisors did not
directly urge child taking,

00:21:35.276 --> 00:21:37.656
and we don't know if they did or didn't,

00:21:37.656 --> 00:21:39.187
it's difficult to believe

00:21:39.187 --> 00:21:42.049
that they never heard anything about it.

00:21:42.049 --> 00:21:44.687
Some children were even
living in military barracks,

00:21:44.687 --> 00:21:49.085
and every child who was adopted
transnationally to the U.S.

00:21:49.085 --> 00:21:53.479
passed through the U.S.
Embassy in San Salvador.

00:21:53.479 --> 00:21:56.080
As Robert White, U.S.
ambassador to EL Salvador

00:21:56.080 --> 00:21:58.782
in 1980 and '81 said of the efforts

00:21:58.782 --> 00:22:02.068
to cover over the horrors of
what the Salvadoran leadership,

00:22:02.068 --> 00:22:05.760
and military was doing by
the Reagan administration,

00:22:05.760 --> 00:22:10.477
and to a lesser extent the Bush
and Carter administrations.

00:22:10.477 --> 00:22:13.321
The Salvadoran military knew that we knew,

00:22:13.321 --> 00:22:16.904
and they knew when we
covered up the truth.

00:22:17.782 --> 00:22:22.647
It was a clear signal that at
a minimum we tolerated this.

00:22:22.647 --> 00:22:25.639
If they knew and didn't speak against it,

00:22:25.639 --> 00:22:29.472
U.S. officials aided and
abetted child taking.

00:22:32.239 --> 00:22:35.310
Although Guatemala shares
a border with El Salvador,

00:22:35.310 --> 00:22:37.707
they're quite different kinds of places,

00:22:37.707 --> 00:22:41.060
and the civil wars had
very different dynamics.

00:22:41.060 --> 00:22:41.992
One could say that about

00:22:41.992 --> 00:22:44.712
the Central America region as a whole.

00:22:44.712 --> 00:22:47.611
Nicaragua, the guerrillas won.

00:22:47.611 --> 00:22:50.411
In El Salvador, they almost won.

00:22:50.411 --> 00:22:53.412
In Guatemala, they had an uphill battle,

00:22:53.412 --> 00:22:55.531
but were stopped by genocide

00:22:55.531 --> 00:22:58.603
when they were beginning to make progress.

00:22:58.603 --> 00:23:02.275
And in Honduras, in part due
to the U.S military presence,

00:23:02.275 --> 00:23:04.775
they never got off the ground.

00:23:05.819 --> 00:23:09.720
Guatemala is much larger than Salvador,

00:23:09.720 --> 00:23:13.801
and its people speak more
than 20 different languages.

00:23:13.801 --> 00:23:15.789
About half are Indigenous,

00:23:15.789 --> 00:23:18.910
many of whom live in remote
communities in the Highlands

00:23:18.910 --> 00:23:21.629
that are a day's walk from any road.

00:23:21.629 --> 00:23:24.389
If El Salvador is finally
coming to a reckoning

00:23:24.389 --> 00:23:26.888
with child taking in the 1980s,

00:23:26.888 --> 00:23:29.939
Guatemala's encounter
with its Cold War horrors

00:23:29.939 --> 00:23:33.689
is still ongoing and
perhaps a losing battle.

00:23:35.668 --> 00:23:38.339
El Salvador has a
relatively complete counting

00:23:38.339 --> 00:23:41.240
of the hundreds of children
that were lost during the war,

00:23:41.240 --> 00:23:43.990
and many of them have been found.

00:23:43.990 --> 00:23:47.418
Guatemala lacks anything
like a comprehensive account

00:23:47.418 --> 00:23:51.751
of the more than 5,000
children who are disappeared.

00:23:52.988 --> 00:23:56.967
There a paranoid Latino ruling
class feared the autonomous,

00:23:56.967 --> 00:24:01.134
non-Spanish speaking
Indigenous Mayan communities.

00:24:02.140 --> 00:24:04.658
Some like the Q'eqchi' speaking Maya

00:24:04.658 --> 00:24:06.868
of a remote region of the highlands.

00:24:06.868 --> 00:24:10.859
That's a picture of somebody
in an Q'eqchi' community,

00:24:10.859 --> 00:24:14.359
were identified as a group as subversives,

00:24:15.651 --> 00:24:17.709
guerrilla sympathizers are worse,

00:24:17.709 --> 00:24:20.310
and targeted for elimination.

00:24:20.310 --> 00:24:22.699
While the military rulers
denied it to the world,

00:24:22.699 --> 00:24:25.687
and most Guatemalans in
the major urban areas

00:24:25.687 --> 00:24:28.025
tried to pretend it wasn't happening,

00:24:28.025 --> 00:24:29.996
the military committed atrocities,

00:24:29.996 --> 00:24:32.362
and massacres in the highlands

00:24:32.362 --> 00:24:35.154
slipping into communities
in the early morning hours,

00:24:35.154 --> 00:24:37.476
and killing everyone,

00:24:37.476 --> 00:24:41.322
destroying crops, killing
animals, burning homes.

00:24:41.322 --> 00:24:43.815
They reveled in gruesome ways

00:24:43.815 --> 00:24:46.476
to end the life in a human body,

00:24:46.476 --> 00:24:49.454
whether of men, women, or children.

00:24:49.454 --> 00:24:53.884
They raised 440 communities
with the intimate,

00:24:53.884 --> 00:24:55.675
and extensive cooperation

00:24:55.675 --> 00:24:58.758
of U.S. military advisors in the CIA.

00:24:59.675 --> 00:25:01.663
Where they didn't assassinate,

00:25:01.663 --> 00:25:05.496
they captured people and
disappeared children.

00:25:06.534 --> 00:25:10.046
The effects of taking small
children and disappearing them

00:25:10.046 --> 00:25:12.164
are similar everywhere.

00:25:12.164 --> 00:25:14.475
The exquisite pain of
the loss of a loved one,

00:25:14.475 --> 00:25:17.954
compounded by the uncertainty
of what happened to them,

00:25:17.954 --> 00:25:20.121
and the agony of having failed someone

00:25:20.121 --> 00:25:23.713
who was so deeply dependent on you.

00:25:23.713 --> 00:25:27.252
Here's a quote from somebody in Guatemala.

00:25:27.252 --> 00:25:30.134
My name is Feliciano Raymundo,

00:25:30.134 --> 00:25:35.123
and I remember that after
they disappeared Jose in 1982,

00:25:35.123 --> 00:25:38.954
they killed my husband and
disappeared my other sons.

00:25:38.954 --> 00:25:42.754
I became ill from the
pure sorrow and suffering.

00:25:42.754 --> 00:25:44.423
During the following 18 years,

00:25:44.423 --> 00:25:46.872
I was able to find three of my sons,

00:25:46.872 --> 00:25:49.932
but I wasn't able to learn about my Jose.

00:25:49.932 --> 00:25:51.742
I don't know what happened.

00:25:51.742 --> 00:25:54.285
Sometimes I thought
that maybe he had died,

00:25:54.285 --> 00:25:56.956
and other times I felt that he was alive.

00:25:56.956 --> 00:25:59.957
I always kept alive that
hope and I wondered,

00:25:59.957 --> 00:26:02.197
who does my son live with?

00:26:02.197 --> 00:26:03.536
Could he be married?

00:26:03.536 --> 00:26:06.403
Where would he be living?

00:26:06.403 --> 00:26:08.676
Other days, sadness would
get the better of me,

00:26:08.676 --> 00:26:10.684
and I would think that
it would've been better

00:26:10.684 --> 00:26:12.655
if I hadn't left him with someone

00:26:12.655 --> 00:26:16.622
in a place where she thought
he would've been safer.

00:26:16.622 --> 00:26:19.456
If I had done other things in those days,

00:26:19.456 --> 00:26:22.539
surely Jose would not have been lost.

00:26:24.257 --> 00:26:27.340
Survivors grieved without resolution.

00:26:28.407 --> 00:26:31.475
The armed forces in the
paramilitaries in Guatemala

00:26:31.475 --> 00:26:34.553
did not kill all the
children they picked up.

00:26:34.553 --> 00:26:35.756
They also took them

00:26:35.756 --> 00:26:38.247
to other Indigenous
communities for adoption,

00:26:38.247 --> 00:26:40.418
or dropped them off at orphanages

00:26:40.418 --> 00:26:42.154
where they made their way to Europe,

00:26:42.154 --> 00:26:45.753
or the United States
and adoption sometimes,

00:26:45.753 --> 00:26:48.939
or took them to towns and cities.

00:26:48.939 --> 00:26:51.962
One of the tactics of the vast
bureaucracy of the military,

00:26:51.962 --> 00:26:53.981
and intelligence services,

00:26:53.981 --> 00:26:56.643
which effectively
encircled the entire nation

00:26:56.643 --> 00:27:01.174
through its network of orejas,
of informants and spies,

00:27:01.174 --> 00:27:04.474
was to pit Indigenous
peoples against each other.

00:27:04.474 --> 00:27:06.996
The goal of disappearing children

00:27:06.996 --> 00:27:11.402
was the logic of ideological
and genocidal elimination

00:27:11.402 --> 00:27:15.065
to disrupt the transmission
of languages, ways of life,

00:27:15.065 --> 00:27:19.148
the practical arts of
weaving huipils or blouses,

00:27:20.345 --> 00:27:24.275
to eliminate ways of thinking and acting.

00:27:24.275 --> 00:27:26.115
Sometimes even during the war,

00:27:26.115 --> 00:27:30.782
people would learn that their
children were still alive,

00:27:32.045 --> 00:27:33.575
but growing up in a community

00:27:33.575 --> 00:27:35.345
that spoke a different language,

00:27:35.345 --> 00:27:38.012
and was loyal to the government.

00:27:38.863 --> 00:27:41.914
On the one hand they
spoke of it being a relief

00:27:41.914 --> 00:27:44.165
to find them alive,

00:27:44.165 --> 00:27:46.012
but they were also full of grief

00:27:46.012 --> 00:27:49.115
to find that their children
were now in some sense

00:27:49.115 --> 00:27:51.254
also part of the enemy

00:27:51.254 --> 00:27:55.837
that might return to kill
them and all their community.

00:27:57.736 --> 00:28:01.454
The Peace Accords were
officially signed in 1996,

00:28:01.454 --> 00:28:06.025
but the civic process of building
peace was never completed.

00:28:06.025 --> 00:28:08.086
The now grown children who were kidnapped

00:28:08.086 --> 00:28:11.384
are still searching for their
families and communities

00:28:11.384 --> 00:28:15.214
while parents and relatives
keep looking for them.

00:28:15.214 --> 00:28:17.174
As elsewhere in Latin America,

00:28:17.174 --> 00:28:21.875
family members of Los desaparecidos
were a political force

00:28:21.875 --> 00:28:26.292
demanding that children who
are taken alive be found.

00:28:27.233 --> 00:28:30.385
Unlike in El Salvador
or Argentina, however,

00:28:30.385 --> 00:28:35.166
in Guatemala the same people
who were found responsible

00:28:35.166 --> 00:28:38.695
for acts of genocide by
the Truth Commissions

00:28:38.695 --> 00:28:40.528
often stayed in power.

00:28:41.585 --> 00:28:43.243
Two reports from the archbishop's

00:28:43.243 --> 00:28:48.076
Commission on Human Rights,
one in 1998, the other in 2000

00:28:48.987 --> 00:28:53.987
collected testimony on first
216 children disappeared alive,

00:28:55.187 --> 00:28:56.770
and later than 444.

00:28:59.227 --> 00:29:01.755
This latter report, "Hasta Encontrarte"

00:29:01.755 --> 00:29:03.754
"Until You Are Found"

00:29:03.754 --> 00:29:06.977
focused exclusively on
disappeared children,

00:29:06.977 --> 00:29:11.786
and suggested that there
were many, many more cases.

00:29:11.786 --> 00:29:15.794
In 1999, a number of groups of
relatives of the disappeared

00:29:15.794 --> 00:29:18.364
came together to begin the massive task

00:29:18.364 --> 00:29:21.466
of trying to reunite
children with the families

00:29:21.466 --> 00:29:26.466
who lost them under the
umbrella of (indistinct)

00:29:26.496 --> 00:29:30.357
By 2008, they had organized 650 reunions

00:29:30.357 --> 00:29:34.066
bringing together translators,
often more than one,

00:29:34.066 --> 00:29:37.624
since many monolingual speakers
of Indigenous languages

00:29:37.624 --> 00:29:39.279
were involved,

00:29:39.279 --> 00:29:41.787
and gathered the resources
for the often long trips

00:29:41.787 --> 00:29:45.120
across the country on buses and on foot.

00:29:46.008 --> 00:29:48.466
These emotional reunions often brought out

00:29:48.466 --> 00:29:51.118
whole communities to celebrate,

00:29:51.118 --> 00:29:53.789
though, the renewed
connections were often fleeting

00:29:53.789 --> 00:29:56.929
in the face of the expense of travel,

00:29:56.929 --> 00:30:00.012
and the absence of a shared language.

00:30:03.689 --> 00:30:05.407
While most of us know something,

00:30:05.407 --> 00:30:08.777
and many of us know a lot
about Central America's wars,

00:30:08.777 --> 00:30:11.253
and the U.S. sponsorship of them,

00:30:11.253 --> 00:30:13.828
now I wanna talk about
the more clandestine war,

00:30:13.828 --> 00:30:17.116
the part that was funded by drugs.

00:30:17.116 --> 00:30:20.175
The Reagan administration
played a much bigger role

00:30:20.175 --> 00:30:23.786
than policy experts
usually want to acknowledge

00:30:23.786 --> 00:30:26.705
in birthing the contemporary
era of drug cartels,

00:30:26.705 --> 00:30:28.854
and drug trafficking,

00:30:28.854 --> 00:30:31.135
although, the general
outlines were laid out

00:30:31.135 --> 00:30:33.277
in the Iran-Contra hearings,

00:30:33.277 --> 00:30:34.186
and subsequently,

00:30:34.186 --> 00:30:38.737
the Kerry Commission Hearings
on drugs and foreign policy.

00:30:38.737 --> 00:30:39.856
Obviously, part of this

00:30:39.856 --> 00:30:43.347
was just destroying the
formal economy through war.

00:30:43.347 --> 00:30:45.287
So-called development loans,

00:30:45.287 --> 00:30:50.256
and bringing to power a ruling
class that shrank the state,

00:30:50.256 --> 00:30:52.868
and often did nothing to build healthcare,

00:30:52.868 --> 00:30:55.187
education or food subsidies,

00:30:55.187 --> 00:30:58.274
leaving the informal
economy, drugs in particular,

00:30:58.274 --> 00:31:00.676
is one of the few ways to make a buck.

00:31:00.676 --> 00:31:03.288
And literally in El
Salvador it's a buck, right?

00:31:03.288 --> 00:31:06.118
They're using U.S. currency,

00:31:06.118 --> 00:31:08.257
but also when Congress cut off funding

00:31:08.257 --> 00:31:11.218
for the anti-communist
wars in Central America,

00:31:11.218 --> 00:31:13.798
it became clear that
the kinds of militaries,

00:31:13.798 --> 00:31:15.319
and paramilitaries

00:31:15.319 --> 00:31:17.917
that the Reagan
administration was supporting

00:31:17.917 --> 00:31:20.340
were engaged in torture, disappearances,

00:31:20.340 --> 00:31:22.869
and bodies in the streets,

00:31:22.869 --> 00:31:25.220
and kidnapping children.

00:31:25.220 --> 00:31:27.379
A shadow U.S. government operation

00:31:27.379 --> 00:31:30.391
allowed them to fund
themselves in shady ways,

00:31:30.391 --> 00:31:34.338
including flying bricks of
cocaine into U.S. cities

00:31:34.338 --> 00:31:35.921
on military planes.

00:31:36.763 --> 00:31:38.900
Indeed, these networks are the origins

00:31:38.900 --> 00:31:40.511
of the current cartels

00:31:40.511 --> 00:31:43.033
that are terrorizing impoverished people,

00:31:43.033 --> 00:31:46.661
especially women and children
throughout Central America,

00:31:46.661 --> 00:31:50.521
and Mexico, and sometimes
the U.S. as well,

00:31:50.521 --> 00:31:55.354
driving the current asylum
crisis on the southwest border.

00:31:58.463 --> 00:32:01.530
The 1980s was also when
children seeking refuge

00:32:01.530 --> 00:32:04.111
from Central America's wars who walked,

00:32:04.111 --> 00:32:06.209
or got a ride across the U.S. border,

00:32:06.209 --> 00:32:09.010
started being held in cages.

00:32:09.010 --> 00:32:12.702
Despite the Trump exceptionalism
that led many to believe

00:32:12.702 --> 00:32:16.785
that child separation for
asylum seekers was new,

00:32:17.697 --> 00:32:21.540
Central American children have
been sent to detention camps,

00:32:21.540 --> 00:32:25.940
and separated from their
parents for decades.

00:32:25.940 --> 00:32:28.730
First Mexico and
secondarily Central America

00:32:28.730 --> 00:32:32.152
came in the 20th century
to function as a kind of

00:32:32.152 --> 00:32:35.432
labor reserve for the United States.

00:32:35.432 --> 00:32:37.484
Agribusiness, service industries,

00:32:37.484 --> 00:32:39.329
and the construction sector

00:32:39.329 --> 00:32:43.071
alongside employers of
housekeepers and nannies

00:32:43.071 --> 00:32:46.261
relied on being able
to access a labor force

00:32:46.261 --> 00:32:48.611
that under the shadow of deportation

00:32:48.611 --> 00:32:51.131
could be expected to work cheaply,

00:32:51.131 --> 00:32:55.232
and outside the system
of labor protections,

00:32:55.232 --> 00:32:56.732
like minimum wage.

00:32:58.811 --> 00:33:00.820
In time of labor oversupply,

00:33:00.820 --> 00:33:04.222
these same workers could be
pushed back into Latin America.

00:33:04.222 --> 00:33:08.323
In times of shortage,
more could be recruited.

00:33:08.323 --> 00:33:11.330
The arrival of large numbers of children

00:33:11.330 --> 00:33:15.423
signaled the death of this
tacit bargain however.

00:33:15.423 --> 00:33:16.610
The deal was essentially

00:33:16.610 --> 00:33:19.501
that workers would not
stay in the United States,

00:33:19.501 --> 00:33:22.810
although that was always a
fantasy more than a reality,

00:33:22.810 --> 00:33:26.220
and employers in local communities
would not pay the costs

00:33:26.220 --> 00:33:29.001
of reproducing the labor force.

00:33:29.001 --> 00:33:31.781
Children would stay in home
countries to get educated,

00:33:31.781 --> 00:33:33.172
get their healthcare,

00:33:33.172 --> 00:33:37.903
and grow to an age where they
could be of use as workers.

00:33:37.903 --> 00:33:41.449
When people became too
old or disabled to work,

00:33:41.449 --> 00:33:44.541
they'd be forced back across the border.

00:33:44.541 --> 00:33:46.262
In this way,

00:33:46.262 --> 00:33:49.121
Latin America functioned like
South Africa's homelands,

00:33:49.121 --> 00:33:52.012
or Bantustans under apartheid,

00:33:52.012 --> 00:33:54.189
a reserve where women,
children, and elders

00:33:54.189 --> 00:33:58.060
were expected to subsist
off a worker's remittances,

00:33:58.060 --> 00:34:01.610
or whatever farming
they could scratch out.

00:34:01.610 --> 00:34:03.143
In the 1980s,

00:34:03.143 --> 00:34:05.810
the system began to
falter in Central America

00:34:05.810 --> 00:34:09.619
as adults and children fled wars at home.

00:34:09.619 --> 00:34:12.529
Women became a larger and
larger group of migrants,

00:34:12.529 --> 00:34:16.279
often bringing children
across the border as well.

00:34:16.279 --> 00:34:19.028
By the 1990s with the Bill Clinton era

00:34:19.028 --> 00:34:21.481
militarization of the border,

00:34:21.481 --> 00:34:23.480
the system broke down even further

00:34:23.480 --> 00:34:26.877
as workers could no longer
return to home countries

00:34:26.877 --> 00:34:29.741
to birth or father children,

00:34:29.741 --> 00:34:32.583
renew relationships with
children or partners,

00:34:32.583 --> 00:34:35.749
or visit elders as they were dying.

00:34:35.749 --> 00:34:38.629
By 2014, when Central American children

00:34:38.629 --> 00:34:42.710
started arriving unaccompanied
in significant numbers,

00:34:42.710 --> 00:34:45.572
it was clear that the
border militarization,

00:34:45.572 --> 00:34:48.500
and deteriorating conditions of violence

00:34:48.500 --> 00:34:51.261
victimizing impoverished
people in the region

00:34:51.261 --> 00:34:54.594
had completely destroyed the old system.

00:34:56.021 --> 00:34:58.228
Unaccompanied children
actually began filing

00:34:58.228 --> 00:35:01.478
asylum petitions in numbers after 2000,

00:35:02.368 --> 00:35:04.077
claiming a human right to safety

00:35:04.077 --> 00:35:07.329
as drug cartels, police
violence and impunity,

00:35:07.329 --> 00:35:10.911
economic collapse and gangs
threaten their wellbeing

00:35:10.911 --> 00:35:12.766
in order to continue to feed

00:35:12.766 --> 00:35:15.300
the apparently insatiable
appetite for drugs

00:35:15.300 --> 00:35:17.050
in the United States.

00:35:17.958 --> 00:35:19.479
U.S. social policy continued

00:35:19.479 --> 00:35:23.136
to try to keep the old
labor agreement in place.

00:35:23.136 --> 00:35:26.297
Its collapse was why conservatives
and white nationalists

00:35:26.297 --> 00:35:29.015
were and are in a dead panic

00:35:29.015 --> 00:35:32.056
about small children crossing the border.

00:35:32.056 --> 00:35:34.325
As soon as there were significant numbers

00:35:34.325 --> 00:35:37.106
of Central Americans
in the U.S. Southwest,

00:35:37.106 --> 00:35:39.016
there were children in detention camps,

00:35:39.016 --> 00:35:41.759
and separated from their parents.

00:35:41.759 --> 00:35:43.309
Most sought to find relatives,

00:35:43.309 --> 00:35:45.361
and disappear into the general population,

00:35:45.361 --> 00:35:46.702
which became easier

00:35:46.702 --> 00:35:50.780
as Central American
immigrant communities grew.

00:35:50.780 --> 00:35:53.863
While many slipped across
the border unnoticed,

00:35:53.863 --> 00:35:57.500
the unlucky ones were
picked up by the INS,

00:35:57.500 --> 00:36:01.468
between 7,400 and 8,500 children a year

00:36:01.468 --> 00:36:03.301
between 1987 and 1993.

00:36:05.301 --> 00:36:07.269
They were strip searched, detained,

00:36:07.269 --> 00:36:09.919
and then usually released
to parents or guardians

00:36:09.919 --> 00:36:13.336
to await hearings on immigration charges.

00:36:14.199 --> 00:36:17.949
There were also for a time
large, sprawling Red Cross camps

00:36:17.949 --> 00:36:19.990
at the Texas Mexico border

00:36:19.990 --> 00:36:23.081
that housed Central American families.

00:36:23.081 --> 00:36:26.222
In 1985, Jenny Lisette Flores

00:36:26.222 --> 00:36:29.571
was part of a group of children
who were held for months,

00:36:29.571 --> 00:36:33.162
or years as a result of new INS rules

00:36:33.162 --> 00:36:36.389
that prevented her release to a guardian.

00:36:36.389 --> 00:36:40.500
The INS, the Immigration
Naturalization Service,

00:36:40.500 --> 00:36:42.023
I just looked around and realized

00:36:42.023 --> 00:36:46.031
that some of you have
only known it as ICE,

00:36:46.031 --> 00:36:49.907
decided it would only
release children in custody

00:36:49.907 --> 00:36:54.452
to a mother, father, or
grandparent, aunt or uncle,

00:36:54.452 --> 00:36:56.501
and only if they could prove

00:36:56.501 --> 00:37:00.184
that they were in status
in the United States.

00:37:00.184 --> 00:37:02.312
If Jenny Flores' mother had picked her up,

00:37:02.312 --> 00:37:04.596
she would essentially
be turning herself in

00:37:04.596 --> 00:37:06.684
as an undocumented immigrant,

00:37:06.684 --> 00:37:09.725
and become subject to deportation.

00:37:09.725 --> 00:37:12.323
Her husband had been
killed in El Salvador,

00:37:12.323 --> 00:37:16.174
and this was not something
she was willing to do.

00:37:16.174 --> 00:37:19.582
So instead she turned to Carlos Holguín,

00:37:19.582 --> 00:37:23.776
an attorney with the National
Center for Immigrant Rights.

00:37:23.776 --> 00:37:27.214
Holguín took the Reagan
administration to court

00:37:27.214 --> 00:37:29.314
on immigrant child detention,

00:37:29.314 --> 00:37:31.535
the conditions under
which children were held,

00:37:31.535 --> 00:37:35.755
and the question of whether
they should be detained at all.

00:37:35.755 --> 00:37:39.234
He sought to get Jenny
Flores released to a cousin

00:37:39.234 --> 00:37:41.284
arguing that she was better off with him

00:37:41.284 --> 00:37:44.274
than in an old hotel
that had been converted

00:37:44.274 --> 00:37:48.154
into an immigration detention facility,

00:37:48.154 --> 00:37:51.583
sharing a room with three
adult male strangers,

00:37:51.583 --> 00:37:55.271
strip searched with no
minimal standards enforced

00:37:55.271 --> 00:37:57.281
for health, sanitation,

00:37:57.281 --> 00:38:02.102
or access to education, or
age appropriate diversions.

00:38:02.102 --> 00:38:05.553
The court and the INS proved
much more willing to clean up

00:38:05.553 --> 00:38:09.010
the conditions of immigrant
detention for children

00:38:09.010 --> 00:38:11.371
than to release them.

00:38:11.371 --> 00:38:14.194
The Flores case ultimately
split into three,

00:38:14.194 --> 00:38:16.542
one on strip searches,

00:38:16.542 --> 00:38:18.879
one on whether refugee
and immigrant children

00:38:18.879 --> 00:38:20.598
could be detained at all.

00:38:20.598 --> 00:38:24.492
And one subsequently on the
conditions of that detention

00:38:24.492 --> 00:38:26.075
in Flores v. Meese.

00:38:27.943 --> 00:38:32.201
In 1987, the Reagan
administration tentatively agreed

00:38:32.201 --> 00:38:36.477
to safer and more humane
conditions in detention,

00:38:36.477 --> 00:38:39.633
the Flores v. Meese settlement agreement.

00:38:39.633 --> 00:38:44.466
The Flores agreement, which
is still in force, supposedly,

00:38:45.536 --> 00:38:49.855
stipulated that children would
only be held for a few days

00:38:49.855 --> 00:38:52.215
in the least restrictive setting,

00:38:52.215 --> 00:38:54.664
a licensed non-secure facility

00:38:54.664 --> 00:38:57.024
to determine whether
they should be deported,

00:38:57.024 --> 00:38:59.596
or reunited with relatives.

00:38:59.596 --> 00:39:02.334
Otherwise, they would be
placed in foster homes,

00:39:02.334 --> 00:39:06.481
or licensed facilities with
age appropriate programs.

00:39:06.481 --> 00:39:09.356
The most important part
of the Flores litigation,

00:39:09.356 --> 00:39:10.273
in my view,

00:39:11.133 --> 00:39:14.477
was the Supreme Court case
to determine whether children

00:39:14.477 --> 00:39:18.256
should be put in immigration
detention at all.

00:39:18.256 --> 00:39:20.465
The Reagan and first Bush administration

00:39:20.465 --> 00:39:23.904
aggressively defended the
practice of holding about

00:39:23.904 --> 00:39:25.737
2,000 children a year.

00:39:26.676 --> 00:39:28.455
They sought to keep detaining children

00:39:28.455 --> 00:39:30.531
even after the Ninth Circuit ruled

00:39:30.531 --> 00:39:33.105
that they had released them to relatives,

00:39:33.105 --> 00:39:37.372
appealing it to the Supreme
Court in Barr v. Flores.

00:39:37.372 --> 00:39:40.370
And, yes, that Barr, Bill Barr,

00:39:40.370 --> 00:39:43.620
also, the attorney general under Trump.

00:39:46.080 --> 00:39:46.957
In that case,

00:39:46.957 --> 00:39:49.435
the government's strategy
was to defend detention

00:39:49.435 --> 00:39:53.234
by arguing it was humane and homelike.

00:39:53.234 --> 00:39:54.834
Advocates for migrant children

00:39:54.834 --> 00:39:56.813
painted a much different picture,

00:39:56.813 --> 00:39:59.475
citing nine different
child advocacy groups

00:39:59.475 --> 00:40:02.555
who testified to shackling, barbed wire,

00:40:02.555 --> 00:40:05.236
arbitrary punishment, inadequate food,

00:40:05.236 --> 00:40:08.236
lack of access to counsel or phones.

00:40:09.943 --> 00:40:12.560
One youth who was subsequently
sent to juvenile hall

00:40:12.560 --> 00:40:15.934
in California described
the vast improvement there

00:40:15.934 --> 00:40:19.191
over INS detention conditions.

00:40:19.191 --> 00:40:22.171
In 1993 by a five-four majority,

00:40:22.171 --> 00:40:25.254
the high court ruled in
favor of keeping children

00:40:25.254 --> 00:40:26.655
in detention,

00:40:26.655 --> 00:40:29.527
with Antonin Scalia
writing for the majority

00:40:29.527 --> 00:40:32.129
that where the conditions
of government custody

00:40:32.129 --> 00:40:34.265
are decent and humane,

00:40:34.265 --> 00:40:39.177
such custody surely does not
violate the Constitution.

00:40:39.177 --> 00:40:40.427
In his dissent,

00:40:42.187 --> 00:40:45.567
Justice Stevens insisted
that on the contrary,

00:40:45.567 --> 00:40:49.396
jailing children for offenses
that were legally as weighty

00:40:49.396 --> 00:40:52.063
as a parking ticket was obscene.

00:40:56.049 --> 00:40:58.876
During the Clinton years little changed.

00:40:58.876 --> 00:41:01.006
About 200 children were being held

00:41:01.006 --> 00:41:04.466
in long term immigrant
detention at any given time,

00:41:04.466 --> 00:41:08.608
some of whom, though not
all, had requested asylum.

00:41:08.608 --> 00:41:11.368
In 1997, a Human Rights Watch report

00:41:11.368 --> 00:41:13.958
damned the practice of jailing children,

00:41:13.958 --> 00:41:17.086
particularly those who were
lawfully present in the U.S.

00:41:17.086 --> 00:41:20.155
because they had petitioned for asylum,

00:41:20.155 --> 00:41:23.406
and called on the INS to
release children to relatives,

00:41:23.406 --> 00:41:25.408
or child welfare agencies

00:41:25.408 --> 00:41:27.808
to inform them of their legal rights,

00:41:27.808 --> 00:41:32.658
and to provide education and
books in detention center.

00:41:32.658 --> 00:41:35.954
The report was embarrassing
to the Clinton administration,

00:41:35.954 --> 00:41:38.595
and so it finally
concluded the negotiations

00:41:38.595 --> 00:41:42.428
over the Flores settlement
begun under Reagan.

00:41:43.326 --> 00:41:44.637
If children must be held,

00:41:44.637 --> 00:41:47.075
it would be under safe
and sanitary conditions

00:41:47.075 --> 00:41:49.548
in a licensed child welfare facility

00:41:49.548 --> 00:41:52.697
with access to food and
water, medical care,

00:41:52.697 --> 00:41:56.418
adequate temperature control and lawyers,

00:41:56.418 --> 00:41:58.926
and segregated from unrelated adults,

00:41:58.926 --> 00:42:03.007
and young prisoners charged with crimes.

00:42:03.007 --> 00:42:05.259
Unfortunately, the Flores agreement

00:42:05.259 --> 00:42:07.205
was never sufficient in itself

00:42:07.205 --> 00:42:10.776
to deter abuse by immigration officials.

00:42:10.776 --> 00:42:13.240
The George W. Bush administration

00:42:13.240 --> 00:42:16.706
detained small children
with their parents,

00:42:16.706 --> 00:42:20.525
and unaccompanied minors
under far harsher conditions

00:42:20.525 --> 00:42:24.760
than the Clinton
administration, or even Reagan.

00:42:24.760 --> 00:42:28.093
Following the September 11, 2001 events,

00:42:29.662 --> 00:42:31.171
the Bush administration

00:42:31.171 --> 00:42:34.879
began separating children from
parents for the first time,

00:42:34.879 --> 00:42:37.340
and parents from each other.

00:42:37.340 --> 00:42:39.148
Instead of family detention,

00:42:39.148 --> 00:42:41.807
they, like the Trump administration,

00:42:41.807 --> 00:42:43.454
subsequently created

00:42:43.454 --> 00:42:47.065
a legal fiction of these
being unaccompanied children

00:42:47.065 --> 00:42:50.546
by virtue of having been
separated from their caregivers

00:42:50.546 --> 00:42:51.463
by the INS,

00:42:53.134 --> 00:42:55.185
which is exactly what
the Trump administration

00:42:55.185 --> 00:42:56.602
subsequently did.

00:42:57.498 --> 00:43:00.847
An Amnesty International report in 2003

00:43:00.847 --> 00:43:04.278
called attention to these conditions,

00:43:04.278 --> 00:43:07.628
and they found the second
Bush administration,

00:43:07.628 --> 00:43:10.057
and customs and border protection,

00:43:10.057 --> 00:43:14.477
essentially ignored the Flores
rules for child detention.

00:43:14.477 --> 00:43:16.518
According to Amnesty International,

00:43:16.518 --> 00:43:19.577
child migrants were
spending years in detention

00:43:19.577 --> 00:43:22.837
without judicial review of their claims.

00:43:22.837 --> 00:43:27.587
To stay in the U.S. strip
searches had been reinstituted.

00:43:28.665 --> 00:43:31.475
They were housed with
juveniles convicted of crimes,

00:43:31.475 --> 00:43:33.138
beaten by guards,

00:43:33.138 --> 00:43:37.344
and subjected to solitary
confinement for infractions

00:43:37.344 --> 00:43:40.094
like poor sportsmanship, swearing,

00:43:40.094 --> 00:43:42.735
or even grammatical mistakes.

00:43:42.735 --> 00:43:44.557
Children as young as seven

00:43:44.557 --> 00:43:47.074
were brought to immigration
hearings in shackles

00:43:47.074 --> 00:43:49.741
with belly chains and leg irons.

00:43:50.626 --> 00:43:53.828
In 2005, the Congressional
Appropriations Committee

00:43:53.828 --> 00:43:57.827
ordered the Bush administration
to stop separating families,

00:43:57.827 --> 00:44:00.146
telling them to use ankle monitors

00:44:00.146 --> 00:44:04.370
to ensure parents appeared
at their asylum hearings.

00:44:04.370 --> 00:44:06.787
Rather than release families, though,

00:44:06.787 --> 00:44:10.939
the Bush administration
expanded family detention.

00:44:10.939 --> 00:44:11.939
Where the previous system

00:44:11.939 --> 00:44:14.418
had relied on a single small facility,

00:44:14.418 --> 00:44:17.178
the Berks Detention
Center in Pennsylvania,

00:44:17.178 --> 00:44:19.544
the Bush administration
opened a much larger one

00:44:19.544 --> 00:44:21.718
to hold more families,

00:44:21.718 --> 00:44:26.135
the T. Don Hutto Detention
Center near Austin, Texas.

00:44:27.375 --> 00:44:31.717
It quickly became a symbol of
the abusiveness of the system.

00:44:31.717 --> 00:44:34.086
A former medium security prison for men,

00:44:34.086 --> 00:44:36.579
it lacked the green space of Berks,

00:44:36.579 --> 00:44:40.558
a former nursing home that
housed up to 84 people,

00:44:40.558 --> 00:44:44.140
and it was characterized
by cells and long hallways

00:44:44.140 --> 00:44:48.830
with bars on the windows with
capacity for more than 500.

00:44:48.830 --> 00:44:51.075
It was a dramatic change in
the treatment of families

00:44:51.075 --> 00:44:52.511
seeking asylum

00:44:52.511 --> 00:44:55.410
who were lawfully present
in the United States,

00:44:55.410 --> 00:44:58.510
who had previously usually been
released into the community

00:44:58.510 --> 00:45:01.079
to await their hearings.

00:45:01.079 --> 00:45:03.837
At Hutto children of refugees

00:45:03.837 --> 00:45:08.678
awaiting hearings on asylum
claims were denied schooling,

00:45:08.678 --> 00:45:12.317
trapped all day in cells
either with unrelated adults,

00:45:12.317 --> 00:45:16.770
or their parents with no
recreational facilities.

00:45:16.770 --> 00:45:20.069
They weren't even allowed
crayons or pajamas.

00:45:20.069 --> 00:45:22.379
The ACLU and others filed lawsuits

00:45:22.379 --> 00:45:24.538
to stop the separation of children,

00:45:24.538 --> 00:45:27.131
and other practices that
had said were prohibited

00:45:27.131 --> 00:45:30.921
under Flores resulting in
a new settlement agreement,

00:45:30.921 --> 00:45:34.801
again, promising to
minimize family detention,

00:45:34.801 --> 00:45:38.384
and the separation of
children and parents.

00:45:43.329 --> 00:45:44.162
Whoops.

00:45:49.087 --> 00:45:52.072
Oh wait, I really do want that slide.

00:45:52.072 --> 00:45:56.161
So in 2020, and particularly
with the COVID-19 crisis,

00:45:56.161 --> 00:45:59.030
the Trump administration
began to actually accomplish

00:45:59.030 --> 00:46:01.076
what it had long set out to win,

00:46:01.076 --> 00:46:03.943
the effective unraveling
of the asylum system

00:46:03.943 --> 00:46:07.429
put in place after World War II.

00:46:07.429 --> 00:46:09.653
First, they learned what
the Obama administration

00:46:09.653 --> 00:46:12.892
understood all along which was that legal,

00:46:12.892 --> 00:46:15.652
and bureaucratic undermining
of the system works better

00:46:15.652 --> 00:46:17.304
than making children cry

00:46:17.304 --> 00:46:20.723
in front of reporters and activists.

00:46:20.723 --> 00:46:25.019
Beginning in 2019, Trump
administration took children

00:46:25.019 --> 00:46:27.001
from their parents seeking asylum

00:46:27.001 --> 00:46:30.481
under the pretext that their
parents were criminals.

00:46:30.481 --> 00:46:33.292
There's not much history in
the U.S. of defending parents

00:46:33.292 --> 00:46:36.881
who've been criminalized no
matter how slight the crime,

00:46:36.881 --> 00:46:39.882
and the mass demonstrations in the street,

00:46:39.882 --> 00:46:42.715
and persistent editorials stopped.

00:46:43.652 --> 00:46:46.401
Then they reactivated
the Obama-era program

00:46:46.401 --> 00:46:49.733
of trying to stop asylum
seekers in Mexico,

00:46:49.733 --> 00:46:51.233
Plan Frontera Sur.

00:46:52.383 --> 00:46:54.930
Officially, ICE gave
some people their due,

00:46:54.930 --> 00:46:57.152
their right to an asylum hearing,

00:46:57.152 --> 00:46:59.973
but metered their crossing
into the United States,

00:46:59.973 --> 00:47:03.301
then sent them back across
the border into Mexico

00:47:03.301 --> 00:47:07.634
to live in UN funded refugee
shelters in tent cities

00:47:08.811 --> 00:47:11.302
to await hearings that never came

00:47:11.302 --> 00:47:13.782
in the midst of the dangerous landscapes

00:47:13.782 --> 00:47:17.951
of the border region where
many were victimized again

00:47:17.951 --> 00:47:20.732
by the same transnational
mafias and cartels

00:47:20.732 --> 00:47:24.102
that they had fled in Central America.

00:47:24.102 --> 00:47:25.509
With the COVID crisis,

00:47:25.509 --> 00:47:28.731
even these limited forms of
access to refugee hearings

00:47:28.731 --> 00:47:31.962
were reduced to the nearest trickle.

00:47:31.962 --> 00:47:35.869
Border patrol agents were
ordered to deport essentially

00:47:35.869 --> 00:47:38.418
any non-citizen found near the border,

00:47:38.418 --> 00:47:43.418
including children under Title
42 of the Public Health Code,

00:47:44.399 --> 00:47:46.661
claiming that their goal
was to avoid exposing

00:47:46.661 --> 00:47:50.577
asylum seekers to COVID
in congregate settings.

00:47:50.577 --> 00:47:54.059
ICE essentially suspended
the asylum system.

00:47:54.059 --> 00:47:57.066
And we will continue to
need an asylum system,

00:47:57.066 --> 00:47:59.498
or something like it

00:47:59.498 --> 00:48:03.161
to address the aftermath
of wars in Central America.

00:48:03.161 --> 00:48:04.748
Decades of intervention

00:48:04.748 --> 00:48:08.061
by international financial institutions,

00:48:08.061 --> 00:48:11.111
and non-governmental
organizations that shrunk,

00:48:11.111 --> 00:48:13.831
and replaced the functions
of federal government

00:48:13.831 --> 00:48:15.389
in Central America

00:48:15.389 --> 00:48:19.509
have by design dramatically
weakened Central American states

00:48:19.509 --> 00:48:22.579
in order to promote free markets.

00:48:22.579 --> 00:48:26.201
Power abhors a vacuum and
the cartels rushed in.

00:48:26.201 --> 00:48:28.547
Local officials, the police, gangs,

00:48:28.547 --> 00:48:30.851
and international crime syndicates,

00:48:30.851 --> 00:48:34.018
increasingly became the same entities,

00:48:34.920 --> 00:48:36.801
often with the endorsement,

00:48:36.801 --> 00:48:38.898
and support of the Trump administration

00:48:38.898 --> 00:48:40.731
during his presidency.

00:48:43.300 --> 00:48:44.972
Murder rates for impoverished people

00:48:44.972 --> 00:48:47.499
at the hands of all
these groups skyrocketed

00:48:47.499 --> 00:48:50.829
as they were increasingly
forced to pay for protection

00:48:50.829 --> 00:48:53.496
to drive a taxi, or run a store.

00:48:54.469 --> 00:48:56.811
People of humble means,
particularly women,

00:48:56.811 --> 00:49:01.040
queer and trans folk, youth
and children fled in droves

00:49:01.040 --> 00:49:04.957
seeking asylum in the
United States and Mexico.

00:49:06.188 --> 00:49:09.149
I wanna conclude by way
of this image of a protest

00:49:09.149 --> 00:49:12.142
at Fort Sill where the
Trump administration

00:49:12.142 --> 00:49:15.392
sought to house 1,600 migrant children.

00:49:16.269 --> 00:49:20.480
Fort Sill began as a POW
camp during the Indian Wars

00:49:20.480 --> 00:49:23.949
where the Apache leader Geronimo died.

00:49:23.949 --> 00:49:25.282
A century later,

00:49:27.589 --> 00:49:32.589
it housed Japanese American
detainees during World War II.

00:49:32.758 --> 00:49:35.437
This group pictured here,

00:49:35.437 --> 00:49:38.208
a coalition of members of
the movement for Black Lives,

00:49:38.208 --> 00:49:40.960
Japanese, Jewish, and Native activists,

00:49:40.960 --> 00:49:44.126
stopped it from becoming
a detention facility

00:49:44.126 --> 00:49:46.599
for migrant children.

00:49:46.599 --> 00:49:49.769
As we watch as the Biden
administration tries to figure out

00:49:49.769 --> 00:49:52.767
whether it will continue to
expel thousands of people,

00:49:52.767 --> 00:49:53.600
and it is,

00:49:54.927 --> 00:49:56.934
or honor its campaign promise

00:49:56.934 --> 00:49:59.116
to listen to what the majority of people

00:49:59.116 --> 00:50:01.534
in the United States actually want,

00:50:01.534 --> 00:50:03.951
which is increased migration,

00:50:05.336 --> 00:50:08.296
a just rapid asylum system,

00:50:08.296 --> 00:50:10.067
and a pathway to citizenship

00:50:10.067 --> 00:50:12.257
rather than a racialized labor system

00:50:12.257 --> 00:50:16.058
that consigns some people to
exploitative jobs in fields,

00:50:16.058 --> 00:50:17.641
and people's homes,

00:50:19.248 --> 00:50:22.238
threatened always with the
risk of losing their children

00:50:22.238 --> 00:50:24.587
in order to keep them in line.

00:50:24.587 --> 00:50:26.368
We need to honor the history of protest

00:50:26.368 --> 00:50:27.958
against child taking,

00:50:27.958 --> 00:50:30.429
and remember that people
in every generation

00:50:30.429 --> 00:50:32.388
have stood against it.

00:50:32.388 --> 00:50:33.796
I'll stop there, thanks.

00:50:33.796 --> 00:50:36.963
(audience applauding)

00:50:40.285 --> 00:50:41.206
- [Stephen] Laura, are you
happy to take questions?

00:50:41.206 --> 00:50:44.539
- I'm more than happy to take questions.

00:51:02.472 --> 00:51:03.651
- [Audience] Hello, great.

00:51:03.651 --> 00:51:07.083
Thank you so much,
professor, this is great.

00:51:07.083 --> 00:51:10.393
I have a question, I guess about,

00:51:10.393 --> 00:51:13.472
I would love to hear your
thoughts on the racialization,

00:51:13.472 --> 00:51:16.573
like the history of the
racialization of families,

00:51:16.573 --> 00:51:19.990
and also the racialization of the border,

00:51:21.043 --> 00:51:24.750
and how this episode of zero
tolerance family separation

00:51:24.750 --> 00:51:28.003
is where those two
histories sort of intersect,

00:51:28.003 --> 00:51:30.473
and if there have been
moments in the past?

00:51:30.473 --> 00:51:32.504
Your lecture, like, speaks to that,

00:51:32.504 --> 00:51:34.894
but I would love to hear your thoughts

00:51:34.894 --> 00:51:38.176
a little bit more on that, yeah.

00:51:38.176 --> 00:51:39.176
- Thank you.

00:51:40.673 --> 00:51:42.455
So the family has always been

00:51:42.455 --> 00:51:45.433
a technology of racialization
in the United States.

00:51:45.433 --> 00:51:50.016
It's remarkable given
all the counterevidence

00:51:50.016 --> 00:51:52.016
in front of us every day

00:51:52.904 --> 00:51:55.987
that we imagine families to be a site

00:51:56.904 --> 00:52:00.323
where people are racially
homogenous, right?

00:52:00.323 --> 00:52:04.951
And the other thing that
we do with the family

00:52:04.951 --> 00:52:09.118
is to see all non-nuclear
families as potentially,

00:52:10.644 --> 00:52:11.977
also, non-white.

00:52:12.943 --> 00:52:14.943
And we know this through

00:52:17.343 --> 00:52:22.115
the image of a figure like
the welfare queen and her kin

00:52:22.115 --> 00:52:24.533
who also turns up as sort of

00:52:24.533 --> 00:52:28.450
the anti-hero of the
culture poverty narrative,

00:52:31.023 --> 00:52:34.773
the figure that centers overpopulation,

00:52:34.773 --> 00:52:38.665
which we were recently reminded
in the El Paso shooting,

00:52:38.665 --> 00:52:43.332
is still very much a live
fantasy of white nationalists.

00:52:44.620 --> 00:52:48.537
Obviously, eugenics in
the '50s, '60s, and '70s

00:52:49.934 --> 00:52:51.851
was a fierce account of

00:52:55.313 --> 00:52:58.264
racialization, not just
of people of color,

00:52:58.264 --> 00:53:00.314
especially earlier in the 20th century,

00:53:00.314 --> 00:53:04.731
but also of white folks of
trying to produce healthy,

00:53:06.227 --> 00:53:09.045
and pure white family, right?

00:53:09.045 --> 00:53:12.045
Without visible signs of disability.

00:53:16.449 --> 00:53:19.640
I've always been interested in

00:53:19.640 --> 00:53:24.223
how we live the context of
empire from inside families,

00:53:26.050 --> 00:53:29.142
how we live the context of racialization,

00:53:29.142 --> 00:53:31.059
how we live the context

00:53:33.191 --> 00:53:36.191
of the economy from within families.

00:53:38.192 --> 00:53:42.910
And one of the things that
so fascinated me about

00:53:42.910 --> 00:53:46.357
what Celeste Ng did in
"Our Missing Hearts"

00:53:46.357 --> 00:53:48.932
which I mentioned at the outset,

00:53:48.932 --> 00:53:51.851
is that she manages to tell the story

00:53:51.851 --> 00:53:56.851
of a dystopic white nationalist
future from inside families.

00:53:58.849 --> 00:54:02.849
And so the question of
how we encounter borders,

00:54:04.558 --> 00:54:06.190
and bordering,

00:54:06.190 --> 00:54:09.211
I think not just in the
U.S. and the Southwest,

00:54:09.211 --> 00:54:12.544
but as also a site where we think family

00:54:15.362 --> 00:54:18.945
is quite interesting
to me, and horrifying,

00:54:20.305 --> 00:54:23.305
as I sort of pursue these questions.

00:54:25.187 --> 00:54:27.628
So we remember the Reagan era

00:54:27.628 --> 00:54:31.961
as the era of traditional
values and anti-gay stuff,

00:54:33.567 --> 00:54:36.150
and fears about single mothers,

00:54:37.717 --> 00:54:40.134
and seeing that play out also

00:54:42.158 --> 00:54:44.741
as punishing immigrant families

00:54:46.367 --> 00:54:49.965
who could not produce
themselves as nuclear, right?

00:54:49.965 --> 00:54:51.568
That only certain people

00:54:51.568 --> 00:54:55.651
could pick up a child from
immigration detention,

00:54:56.496 --> 00:54:58.438
and they had to be

00:54:58.438 --> 00:55:01.799
a pretty near approximation
of the nuclear family,

00:55:01.799 --> 00:55:03.966
not a cousin, for example.

00:55:04.937 --> 00:55:07.976
And we have a long history, too,

00:55:07.976 --> 00:55:11.884
of encountering in the United States,

00:55:11.884 --> 00:55:16.884
the encounter between tribal
nations and U.S. racism

00:55:17.000 --> 00:55:21.500
has long been in terms of
the problem of understanding

00:55:23.548 --> 00:55:28.548
a tribal community as not
composed of nuclear families.

00:55:28.565 --> 00:55:32.897
And over and over again seeing
it sexualized and racialized,

00:55:32.897 --> 00:55:35.564
and turned into an object of war

00:55:37.095 --> 00:55:42.016
through specifically thinking
about patterns of sexuality,

00:55:42.016 --> 00:55:44.136
and family making.

00:55:44.136 --> 00:55:48.886
And, of course, the history
of enslavement is the history

00:55:50.536 --> 00:55:52.954
of slavers telling the story

00:55:52.954 --> 00:55:57.067
that Black mothers had no
feelings for their children,

00:55:57.067 --> 00:56:00.400
and that's why they could be sold apart.

00:56:02.535 --> 00:56:06.535
So that's a really long
answer to your question.

00:56:12.845 --> 00:56:15.065
- [Audience] Do you want
me to stand or can I sit?

00:56:15.065 --> 00:56:18.627
Hi, so I had a question and
maybe a little bit outside of,

00:56:18.627 --> 00:56:22.867
I don't know, maybe related
more than I think, but,

00:56:22.867 --> 00:56:24.336
so recently there have been, like,

00:56:24.336 --> 00:56:25.697
all these new technologies

00:56:25.697 --> 00:56:30.245
through which some of these
children now as adults

00:56:30.245 --> 00:56:33.215
have tried to reclaim those connections,

00:56:33.215 --> 00:56:35.627
and I'm thinking of in Argentina

00:56:35.627 --> 00:56:38.993
of all the children
disappear, DNA, and all that.

00:56:38.993 --> 00:56:41.918
Can you just talk about
what you've encounter,

00:56:41.918 --> 00:56:43.790
and what you've read about that,

00:56:43.790 --> 00:56:45.347
and your thoughts on it?

00:56:45.347 --> 00:56:46.180
- Sure.

00:56:47.747 --> 00:56:50.247
So it's a transnational effort

00:56:53.238 --> 00:56:56.008
by Physicians for Human Rights

00:56:56.008 --> 00:57:00.341
to actually develop technology
that would allow them

00:57:01.197 --> 00:57:02.280
to transform.

00:57:04.759 --> 00:57:06.498
In the '80s we had good technology

00:57:06.498 --> 00:57:10.338
for being able to identify through DNA

00:57:10.338 --> 00:57:12.598
who was your child, right?

00:57:12.598 --> 00:57:14.219
That's still the only thing DNA,

00:57:14.219 --> 00:57:16.837
by the way, is really good at.

00:57:16.837 --> 00:57:19.436
All the stuff where people tell you

00:57:19.436 --> 00:57:22.353
you can send your DNA to some bank,

00:57:24.267 --> 00:57:26.528
and they'll tell you
what your ethnicity is.

00:57:26.528 --> 00:57:29.259
That's not what DNA is good for.

00:57:29.259 --> 00:57:32.946
However, those banks are collecting

00:57:32.946 --> 00:57:35.227
tons and tons of information

00:57:35.227 --> 00:57:37.340
that is being used by law enforcement

00:57:37.340 --> 00:57:41.090
to tell stories about
families and relations.

00:57:42.758 --> 00:57:46.925
And so to try to solve
crimes through identifying,

00:57:48.126 --> 00:57:49.887
oh, it must be this person's son,

00:57:49.887 --> 00:57:52.137
this person's half-brother,

00:57:53.824 --> 00:57:54.741
but so what

00:57:58.114 --> 00:58:01.214
Physicians for Human Rights sought to do

00:58:01.214 --> 00:58:04.583
was to figure out if you
could identify the grandmother

00:58:04.583 --> 00:58:08.877
of somebody, particularly
because it was groups

00:58:08.877 --> 00:58:12.563
identifying as grandmothers
that were at the forefront

00:58:12.563 --> 00:58:16.980
of the activism to recover
Argentina's lost children.

00:58:18.594 --> 00:58:21.706
And indeed they began to be able to

00:58:21.706 --> 00:58:24.206
trace out those relationships.

00:58:28.725 --> 00:58:30.847
It was an amazing effort

00:58:30.847 --> 00:58:33.535
that brought together people in the U.S.,

00:58:33.535 --> 00:58:35.696
people in Argentina,

00:58:35.696 --> 00:58:38.696
and people throughout Latin America,

00:58:39.729 --> 00:58:43.967
who developed these technologies together.

00:58:43.967 --> 00:58:48.550
So one of the things that
feels particularly grim to me

00:58:50.149 --> 00:58:52.390
in the United States right now,

00:58:52.390 --> 00:58:54.723
is how difficult it feels to

00:58:57.308 --> 00:59:02.308
find sources of opposition to
the growing white nationalism.

00:59:03.980 --> 00:59:07.548
And the fascinating thing is everybody,

00:59:07.548 --> 00:59:09.291
this is a side note, sorry,

00:59:09.291 --> 00:59:12.722
but everybody keeps talking about,

00:59:12.722 --> 00:59:14.940
oh, we have to think
about Hungary and Poland.

00:59:14.940 --> 00:59:16.991
And sure we can think
about Hungary and Poland,

00:59:16.991 --> 00:59:19.102
but it's as if we don't live

00:59:19.102 --> 00:59:22.429
in the hemisphere of the Americas.

00:59:22.429 --> 00:59:26.750
Like it's as if we don't know
how much these ideologies

00:59:26.750 --> 00:59:30.621
were developed for the U.S. ruling class

00:59:30.621 --> 00:59:33.893
in relationship to Latin America.

00:59:33.893 --> 00:59:35.954
Even this anti-trans
stuff people are like,

00:59:35.954 --> 00:59:37.531
oh, my God, it's just like, I'm great.

00:59:37.531 --> 00:59:40.570
No, oh, my God, it's just like Columbia.

00:59:40.570 --> 00:59:43.634
Oh, my God, it's just like Latin America.

00:59:43.634 --> 00:59:45.731
And these people know each other.

00:59:45.731 --> 00:59:49.163
These people are often
related to each other.

00:59:49.163 --> 00:59:51.746
Anyway, so it's important to me

00:59:53.403 --> 00:59:57.533
to stay in touch with
Latin American activists

00:59:57.533 --> 01:00:01.300
who have faced all of this for decades

01:00:01.300 --> 01:00:05.383
with tremendous resources
and tremendous courage.

01:00:06.523 --> 01:00:09.814
I don't mean to be flip about this,

01:00:09.814 --> 01:00:14.814
but we don't get killed much
in demonstrations, and they do.

01:00:16.414 --> 01:00:19.252
I think they've faced
much harsher conditions

01:00:19.252 --> 01:00:24.234
than we have for their activism
with much greater courage

01:00:24.234 --> 01:00:29.234
than in general people in the
United States have expressed.

01:00:29.534 --> 01:00:32.085
And that's not to minimize, actually,

01:00:32.085 --> 01:00:35.832
the sheer level of violence
that people encountered

01:00:35.832 --> 01:00:39.733
in the summer of 2020 in the
Black Lives Matter protests,

01:00:39.733 --> 01:00:42.460
which I still think we don't have a full,

01:00:42.460 --> 01:00:44.852
I mean, we have the reports
that tell us that story,

01:00:44.852 --> 01:00:49.182
but we don't have that
as a shared narrative

01:00:49.182 --> 01:00:51.923
that people were hurt and killed regularly

01:00:51.923 --> 01:00:55.592
in those demonstrations by the police,

01:00:55.592 --> 01:00:57.009
but by and large,

01:00:58.965 --> 01:01:01.642
those kinds of experiences

01:01:01.642 --> 01:01:03.742
are more the exception than the rule

01:01:03.742 --> 01:01:08.453
in how protest has been
treated in the United States

01:01:08.453 --> 01:01:12.703
compared to how it's been
treated in Latin America.

01:01:15.569 --> 01:01:16.743
- [Audience] Thank you.

01:01:16.743 --> 01:01:18.951
- [Audience] Thank you
very much for your talk,

01:01:18.951 --> 01:01:21.250
even though you are
telling a very horrific,

01:01:21.250 --> 01:01:22.917
and outraging story.

01:01:25.207 --> 01:01:27.882
I was just wondering
what about the families

01:01:27.882 --> 01:01:30.632
that have adopted these children?

01:01:33.428 --> 01:01:35.239
Do they know what were the conditions

01:01:35.239 --> 01:01:37.366
that allowed them to adopt?

01:01:37.366 --> 01:01:39.239
And are they openly,

01:01:39.239 --> 01:01:42.188
or consciously complicit with this story,

01:01:42.188 --> 01:01:44.527
or what's the story there?

01:01:44.527 --> 01:01:46.469
- Yeah, thanks for that question.

01:01:46.469 --> 01:01:47.319
I went back and forth

01:01:47.319 --> 01:01:50.902
about whether to talk
about it in the talk.

01:01:54.536 --> 01:01:58.137
So when I was in El Salvador
talking to the lawyers,

01:01:58.137 --> 01:02:01.137
and the researchers at Pro-Búsqueda,

01:02:02.038 --> 01:02:06.288
they talked about what it
meant to contact families

01:02:07.187 --> 01:02:08.937
in the United States.

01:02:12.827 --> 01:02:16.316
Obviously, humans are incredibly diverse,

01:02:16.316 --> 01:02:19.733
and there are many different experiences.

01:02:21.085 --> 01:02:24.878
For example, I'm not thinking of her name,

01:02:24.878 --> 01:02:28.375
the title of her book is "Missing Mila."

01:02:28.375 --> 01:02:31.647
One adoptive parent of a Salvadoran kid

01:02:31.647 --> 01:02:36.647
actually reached out to the
family in Salvador and Honduras,

01:02:38.209 --> 01:02:40.042
and fully acknowledged

01:02:43.489 --> 01:02:45.656
what was her kid's kind of

01:02:47.799 --> 01:02:51.132
difficult to understand account of that.

01:02:53.430 --> 01:02:55.671
And she very much had,

01:02:55.671 --> 01:02:59.032
or he very much had parents
alive in El Salvador,

01:02:59.032 --> 01:03:02.213
and relatives alive in El Salvador.

01:03:02.213 --> 01:03:07.046
We also know the story from
the film "Discovering Dominga"

01:03:08.094 --> 01:03:13.011
of a Guatemalan young person
whose sister was a missionary,

01:03:16.352 --> 01:03:18.321
so had good Spanish,

01:03:18.321 --> 01:03:20.619
and a lot of knowledge of Guatemala,

01:03:20.619 --> 01:03:21.786
and as Dominga

01:03:24.701 --> 01:03:28.500
kept talking about her
experiences, and what she knew,

01:03:28.500 --> 01:03:32.000
her sister was able to find her community,

01:03:34.343 --> 01:03:37.120
and her surviving relatives.

01:03:37.120 --> 01:03:39.453
So I say that as scaffolding

01:03:40.322 --> 01:03:43.381
to say what the lawyers at
Pro-Búsqueda had told me

01:03:43.381 --> 01:03:45.669
is that the overwhelming
majority of U.S. families

01:03:45.669 --> 01:03:49.759
don't even wanna
acknowledge what happened,

01:03:49.759 --> 01:03:54.342
and only a handful of them
are willing to have contact.

01:03:58.435 --> 01:04:02.352
I've been starting to
think more and more about

01:04:04.083 --> 01:04:07.424
how foster care was built
at such a homophobic,

01:04:07.424 --> 01:04:10.462
and transphobic institution,

01:04:10.462 --> 01:04:13.673
why it's one of the most,
like, explicitly homophobic,

01:04:13.673 --> 01:04:17.310
and transphobic wings of the state.

01:04:17.310 --> 01:04:21.977
And I think it's related to
why people may be really bad

01:04:23.054 --> 01:04:28.054
at figuring out a relationship
to these imperial relations,

01:04:28.284 --> 01:04:29.951
which is to say that

01:04:33.964 --> 01:04:37.797
evangelical Christians
have embraced adoption,

01:04:38.971 --> 01:04:42.164
embraced transnational
adoption, so have Mormons,

01:04:42.164 --> 01:04:43.113
but they've embraced it

01:04:43.113 --> 01:04:46.863
within a particular
narrative of exclusivity.

01:04:48.184 --> 01:04:50.915
And while that's certainly
not all the people

01:04:50.915 --> 01:04:54.034
who are involved in
transnational adoption,

01:04:54.034 --> 01:04:57.144
it's a disproportionate number of them.

01:04:57.144 --> 01:04:58.727
And while sometimes

01:04:59.634 --> 01:05:03.184
the experience of understanding the ways,

01:05:03.184 --> 01:05:05.553
and this is what I keep thinking about,

01:05:05.553 --> 01:05:06.864
and why I keep writing about it,

01:05:06.864 --> 01:05:09.662
the ways that the insides of families

01:05:09.662 --> 01:05:12.553
become complicit in U.S. empire.

01:05:12.553 --> 01:05:14.353
Sometimes that's quite disturbing,

01:05:14.353 --> 01:05:18.353
and provocative to people
in the U.S. or Europe,

01:05:20.321 --> 01:05:22.154
but for the most part,

01:05:23.514 --> 01:05:27.042
all the energy that goes into disavowing

01:05:27.042 --> 01:05:31.125
U.S. empire in general is
replicated by families.

01:05:37.855 --> 01:05:40.882
- [Audience] Hi, I'm
a history PhD student,

01:05:40.882 --> 01:05:44.030
and I've noticed in the last several years

01:05:44.030 --> 01:05:46.553
in that discipline there's
been a move towards

01:05:46.553 --> 01:05:49.323
using liberalism as a
framework for critiquing

01:05:49.323 --> 01:05:52.047
some of the systems of
society that we least like.

01:05:52.047 --> 01:05:52.880
- Can you talk.

01:05:52.880 --> 01:05:53.713
- [Audience] Yes.

01:05:53.713 --> 01:05:54.546
- Yeah, I'm losing you.

01:05:54.546 --> 01:05:55.379
- [Audience] Sorry.

01:05:55.379 --> 01:05:57.239
For example, like, there
are some scholars who are,

01:05:57.239 --> 01:06:00.428
like, carcerality is a
symptom of liberalism

01:06:00.428 --> 01:06:01.909
in the liberal state.

01:06:01.909 --> 01:06:03.868
Of course, the challenge
with that argument

01:06:03.868 --> 01:06:05.212
is that there's non-liberal states

01:06:05.212 --> 01:06:06.467
that are extremely carceral,

01:06:06.467 --> 01:06:08.251
like China and Russia, for example.

01:06:08.251 --> 01:06:11.540
And so I'm just curious if
you think that liberalism

01:06:11.540 --> 01:06:14.271
is a useful framework for addressing,

01:06:14.271 --> 01:06:16.897
for criticizing the
issues that you've raised?

01:06:16.897 --> 01:06:20.378
Is taking children something
that liberal regimes do,

01:06:20.378 --> 01:06:22.012
and that's the right entry point

01:06:22.012 --> 01:06:22.990
for thinking about these things,

01:06:22.990 --> 01:06:24.990
or is that too limiting?

01:06:28.879 --> 01:06:30.739
- As I was giving this talk,

01:06:30.739 --> 01:06:33.602
I kind of tripped over
the part where I said

01:06:33.602 --> 01:06:35.935
we need a good asylum system

01:06:37.012 --> 01:06:40.231
because the other way to
say it is we need to abolish

01:06:40.231 --> 01:06:43.871
the distinction between
immigrants and asylum seekers.

01:06:43.871 --> 01:06:46.038
And that it seems to me is

01:06:49.329 --> 01:06:52.300
certainly much closer to what I hope for,

01:06:52.300 --> 01:06:55.128
but it seems further away.

01:06:55.128 --> 01:06:57.911
And so that's not what I said,

01:06:57.911 --> 01:06:59.671
or that's not what I wrote.

01:06:59.671 --> 01:07:03.088
And it seems to me that that encapsulates

01:07:04.448 --> 01:07:09.200
the problem with using
liberalism right there, right?

01:07:09.200 --> 01:07:12.647
Liberalism is a widely shared framework

01:07:12.647 --> 01:07:17.064
that we can use to develop
certain kinds of arguments

01:07:17.899 --> 01:07:20.442
that are useful to certain people,

01:07:20.442 --> 01:07:25.025
but at the end of the day
it's also the problem, right?

01:07:27.901 --> 01:07:32.728
And so I think when you raise
the issue of carcerality

01:07:32.728 --> 01:07:35.409
that's precisely the point

01:07:35.409 --> 01:07:39.742
that liberalism with its
relationship to capitalism,

01:07:40.989 --> 01:07:45.572
and its relationship to a
certain account of the family

01:07:48.470 --> 01:07:50.220
gives us this system.

01:07:51.465 --> 01:07:56.382
And so everyone's always an
exception in liberalism, right?

01:07:57.384 --> 01:08:01.072
And we've seen the asylum
and immigration systems

01:08:01.072 --> 01:08:04.072
very much operate within the system.

01:08:06.564 --> 01:08:08.064
It used to be that

01:08:10.306 --> 01:08:15.034
people who were approved for
asylum were approved in blocks,

01:08:15.034 --> 01:08:15.893
right?

01:08:15.893 --> 01:08:17.226
Cambodians were,

01:08:20.252 --> 01:08:22.763
because what we understood was that people

01:08:22.763 --> 01:08:25.346
belonged to a common situation.

01:08:31.494 --> 01:08:34.963
I'm now stumbling 'cause
I don't wanna, like,

01:08:34.963 --> 01:08:39.963
what conservatives operating
within capital "L" liberalism

01:08:39.993 --> 01:08:43.160
understood is that liberalism's genius

01:08:44.512 --> 01:08:46.982
is to turn everybody into an individual

01:08:46.982 --> 01:08:49.982
over and over and over again, right?

01:08:52.130 --> 01:08:55.630
I'm thinking about Thatcher saying, right?

01:08:56.511 --> 01:09:00.353
There are individuals,
and there are families.

01:09:00.353 --> 01:09:04.829
And one of the things that
fascinates me about that line,

01:09:04.829 --> 01:09:07.746
so just to finish out the previous.

01:09:09.819 --> 01:09:14.007
So now, like, zillions of
lawyers have to be deployed

01:09:14.007 --> 01:09:17.090
to each and every single asylum case,

01:09:19.218 --> 01:09:21.946
each and every immigration case.

01:09:21.946 --> 01:09:25.437
And they're never gonna be enough of us.

01:09:25.437 --> 01:09:28.429
They're never enough expert witnesses,

01:09:28.429 --> 01:09:30.346
just we've lost, right?

01:09:32.698 --> 01:09:35.538
Once everybody becomes an individual,

01:09:35.538 --> 01:09:39.349
and not that one shouldn't
care about those individuals,

01:09:39.349 --> 01:09:41.766
of course, and their stories,

01:09:43.688 --> 01:09:48.271
but that this is how the
system made it so exceptional,

01:09:49.847 --> 01:09:53.514
that asylum seekers
would be granted asylum,

01:09:54.819 --> 01:09:58.539
that people would be approved as refugees,

01:09:58.539 --> 01:10:03.039
that people would be approved
for an immigration visa,

01:10:05.367 --> 01:10:06.698
but the other thing that's fascinating

01:10:06.698 --> 01:10:11.508
about the Thatcher line
is how much our households

01:10:11.508 --> 01:10:13.528
are important to liberalism.

01:10:13.528 --> 01:10:16.939
And I could say a lot about that,

01:10:16.939 --> 01:10:19.022
and I'm gonna try not to,

01:10:19.937 --> 01:10:20.937
but the ways

01:10:24.149 --> 01:10:26.982
that we are embedded in households

01:10:28.819 --> 01:10:31.330
only as nuclear families, right?

01:10:31.330 --> 01:10:33.907
Only nuclear families count

01:10:33.907 --> 01:10:38.490
is another way of exceptionalizing
all sorts of people.

01:10:40.889 --> 01:10:43.328
And indeed the majority
of people in the world

01:10:43.328 --> 01:10:45.867
are not in nuclear
families it's ridiculous.

01:10:45.867 --> 01:10:49.778
So anyway, individuals and their families

01:10:49.778 --> 01:10:52.028
is how liberalism operates.

01:10:54.335 --> 01:10:55.989
- [Stephen] Laura, I wanted
just to follow-up on that.

01:10:55.989 --> 01:11:00.989
Part of your title of your talk
is about tactics of terror.

01:11:01.370 --> 01:11:05.559
And it strikes me that you've
put a lot of focus today

01:11:05.559 --> 01:11:06.789
in the actual talk

01:11:06.789 --> 01:11:09.348
about the terror throughout Latin America,

01:11:09.348 --> 01:11:12.658
much of it related to U.S. empire.

01:11:12.658 --> 01:11:15.628
In the the book you also
talk about terror tactics

01:11:15.628 --> 01:11:17.576
against particular racialized communities

01:11:17.576 --> 01:11:18.478
within the United States

01:11:18.478 --> 01:11:21.586
that you didn't focus as
much attention on here,

01:11:21.586 --> 01:11:24.419
Native, Black, Latino, and others.

01:11:25.548 --> 01:11:27.113
And I just wanted to invite you

01:11:27.113 --> 01:11:28.857
to talk a little bit about that now.

01:11:28.857 --> 01:11:31.928
It may relate to this question
of individuation and family,

01:11:31.928 --> 01:11:34.035
or it may say something else about

01:11:34.035 --> 01:11:35.377
the treatment of people as groups

01:11:35.377 --> 01:11:39.355
at moments of emerging
political influence,

01:11:39.355 --> 01:11:40.855
or radical threat.

01:11:41.939 --> 01:11:44.856
- Yes, thank you for that question.

01:11:46.176 --> 01:11:49.426
So the thing I've been trying to argue,

01:11:50.936 --> 01:11:53.014
or the thing I tried to argue in the book

01:11:53.014 --> 01:11:54.931
is that what we can see

01:11:56.622 --> 01:12:00.455
is the use of the tactic
of taking children is

01:12:04.419 --> 01:12:07.419
that it's always a political tactic.

01:12:08.610 --> 01:12:11.661
It is actually not about individuals.

01:12:11.661 --> 01:12:15.197
And so I talk about the
civil rights movement,

01:12:15.197 --> 01:12:18.780
and the moment of the
explosion in the size

01:12:23.277 --> 01:12:25.338
of the foster care system

01:12:25.338 --> 01:12:29.268
being in the context of
school desegregation.

01:12:29.268 --> 01:12:32.766
And so at the very moment
that Black communities

01:12:32.766 --> 01:12:34.683
are using as a strategy

01:12:36.435 --> 01:12:41.353
to sort of present themselves
in their Sunday best

01:12:41.353 --> 01:12:42.770
in demonstrations

01:12:43.807 --> 01:12:48.724
as respectable people deserving
of citizenship and respect.

01:12:50.242 --> 01:12:52.845
The tactic of white supremacists

01:12:52.845 --> 01:12:55.678
was to paint them as disreputable,

01:12:57.350 --> 01:13:02.267
particularly to focus on single
mothers and their children.

01:13:08.150 --> 01:13:12.400
Cutting off welfare was a
strategy to crush support

01:13:13.328 --> 01:13:16.860
for civil rights or
terrorized communities.

01:13:16.860 --> 01:13:21.193
And that in turn became a
way of separating children

01:13:22.360 --> 01:13:24.351
from their parents

01:13:24.351 --> 01:13:29.190
by basically starving single
mothers and their children.

01:13:29.190 --> 01:13:31.889
And then the community would say,

01:13:31.889 --> 01:13:35.117
well, you can't take care of your child.

01:13:35.117 --> 01:13:38.408
And so that child should
be placed in foster care.

01:13:38.408 --> 01:13:43.180
And the federal government
actually pioneered that strategy

01:13:43.180 --> 01:13:46.263
in conversation with Southern states.

01:13:48.721 --> 01:13:50.888
I also talk about the ways

01:13:52.568 --> 01:13:56.999
that a certain kind of abortion
politics were pioneered

01:13:56.999 --> 01:14:00.832
in relationship to the
so-called war on drugs.

01:14:02.187 --> 01:14:07.187
And so the accusation that
pregnant women were using drugs,

01:14:07.217 --> 01:14:09.459
or especially in Native communities,

01:14:09.459 --> 01:14:13.292
that pregnant women were
drinking excessively.

01:14:14.217 --> 01:14:17.300
Both of these were embarrassing lies.

01:14:20.406 --> 01:14:25.406
I wanna say as often as I can
because it still is troubling.

01:14:28.030 --> 01:14:30.698
Cocaine has no affect on a pregnancy.

01:14:30.698 --> 01:14:35.365
This hysteria about crack
babies was entirely ginned up,

01:14:38.560 --> 01:14:41.808
but what prosecutors were doing,

01:14:41.808 --> 01:14:44.328
ambitious anti-abortion
prosecutors were doing,

01:14:44.328 --> 01:14:48.159
was trying to produce an
account of fetal personhood

01:14:48.159 --> 01:14:51.921
that we now see running
rampant through the land

01:14:51.921 --> 01:14:55.838
as an account of why
women can't have autonomy.

01:14:58.038 --> 01:15:02.739
Women and trans folks can't
have autonomy over their bodies,

01:15:02.739 --> 01:15:05.150
even if there's a six-week fetus

01:15:05.150 --> 01:15:07.733
the size of a lentil inside it.

01:15:09.721 --> 01:15:14.721
If you're carrying around
a person inside your body,

01:15:14.871 --> 01:15:16.371
you become subject

01:15:17.661 --> 01:15:21.494
to the protection of
that person by the state,

01:15:23.471 --> 01:15:25.971
and that was always the point.

01:15:27.052 --> 01:15:29.385
And sometimes people ask me,

01:15:30.271 --> 01:15:35.271
so why was the state trying to
save producing this rhetoric

01:15:35.522 --> 01:15:39.629
about saving Black babies
and saving Native babies?

01:15:39.629 --> 01:15:44.379
And it seems to me that it
never particularly cared about

01:15:47.013 --> 01:15:49.280
saving Black babies, or
saving Native babies,

01:15:49.280 --> 01:15:51.363
so much as it cared about

01:15:52.659 --> 01:15:56.699
depriving Black and
Native women of rights,

01:15:56.699 --> 01:16:01.199
and sending them to prison
and jail for being pregnant

01:16:02.215 --> 01:16:04.048
as a sort of trial run

01:16:06.498 --> 01:16:11.498
for how to push anti-abortion
politics in the United States.

01:16:13.830 --> 01:16:16.857
And I talk also about the fights

01:16:16.857 --> 01:16:20.349
for the Indian Child Welfare Act,

01:16:20.349 --> 01:16:22.932
and laws to limit, or not laws,

01:16:24.879 --> 01:16:29.869
but policies to limit the
placement of Black foster kids

01:16:29.869 --> 01:16:32.699
in white adoptive homes.

01:16:32.699 --> 01:16:36.448
And the fights against those in the 1990s,

01:16:36.448 --> 01:16:41.448
and still the Fifth Circuit
just handed down a decision

01:16:41.746 --> 01:16:45.276
that seems to threaten the
Indian Child Welfare Act.

01:16:45.276 --> 01:16:47.943
And I wanna say that's so crazy.

01:16:49.870 --> 01:16:52.439
Like you can't do that, right?

01:16:52.439 --> 01:16:56.978
The claim that what the
Indian Child Welfare Act does

01:16:56.978 --> 01:17:00.960
is produce Native people
as a racial group,

01:17:00.960 --> 01:17:02.627
and that's improper.

01:17:03.880 --> 01:17:05.199
It does no such thing.

01:17:05.199 --> 01:17:08.942
It produces Native people as
members of a particular polity,

01:17:08.942 --> 01:17:11.749
and as members of tribal nations.

01:17:11.749 --> 01:17:13.011
It's very explicit about that,

01:17:13.011 --> 01:17:16.663
but what the last few years have taught me

01:17:16.663 --> 01:17:20.851
is to stop believing that
crazy s**t doesn't happen

01:17:20.851 --> 01:17:22.018
in the courts.

01:17:23.998 --> 01:17:25.869
Sorry, I couldn't get
through that sentence

01:17:25.869 --> 01:17:30.619
without the curse, but, so
that's some of the rest of it.

01:17:34.633 --> 01:17:37.796
I point out what I think
we don't say enough,

01:17:37.796 --> 01:17:41.296
which is Indian boarding schools

01:17:41.296 --> 01:17:44.213
were started by the war department.

01:17:45.095 --> 01:17:49.565
They were started as a strategy
to address Indian wars.

01:17:49.565 --> 01:17:52.353
They were meant to terrorize people,

01:17:52.353 --> 01:17:56.020
and then they were
subsequently supplemented

01:17:57.834 --> 01:18:01.667
with religious schools
by the Catholic church,

01:18:02.664 --> 01:18:05.914
the Mormons and other Christian groups,

01:18:07.041 --> 01:18:10.162
but it's important to remember that,

01:18:10.162 --> 01:18:12.286
like, Carlisle Indian School,

01:18:12.286 --> 01:18:16.326
most famous of Indian
schools, what is it now?

01:18:16.326 --> 01:18:18.409
It's an Army War College.

01:18:19.655 --> 01:18:22.674
It never stopped being
a military facility.

01:18:22.674 --> 01:18:26.507
And in fact, the legal
basis under which Trump

01:18:27.623 --> 01:18:30.040
could give an executive order

01:18:32.223 --> 01:18:36.473
for asylum seekers to lose
their kids at the border

01:18:37.445 --> 01:18:40.052
without being legally challenged

01:18:40.052 --> 01:18:43.723
was precisely the same basis under which

01:18:43.723 --> 01:18:48.640
the Indian boarding schools
were enacted as federal policy.

01:18:49.763 --> 01:18:53.523
So these things are very
much tied up with each other,

01:18:53.523 --> 01:18:56.606
and we are still living their legacy.

01:18:58.213 --> 01:19:01.234
Queer families are
losing their kids again.

01:19:01.234 --> 01:19:02.651
They always were,

01:19:04.856 --> 01:19:06.606
but it was for a time

01:19:09.306 --> 01:19:11.146
Black and Latinx queer families

01:19:11.146 --> 01:19:13.625
were mostly losing their kids.

01:19:13.625 --> 01:19:17.292
And now it's very white
middle class parents

01:19:18.913 --> 01:19:22.080
in Texas of trans kids who are saying,

01:19:23.996 --> 01:19:28.263
wait, I'm the sort of person
you want to be a foster parent.

01:19:28.263 --> 01:19:29.914
I'm sorry, there are
people I follow on Twitter,

01:19:29.914 --> 01:19:33.914
and I'm like, please stop,
please don't do that,

01:19:37.322 --> 01:19:42.322
but it's actually fascinating
to find coalitions in Texas

01:19:42.894 --> 01:19:45.811
between the anti-carceral activists

01:19:47.254 --> 01:19:51.236
who wanna end what they call
the family regulation system,

01:19:51.236 --> 01:19:55.319
and the foster care system
with very conservative

01:19:57.974 --> 01:20:00.736
evangelicals who wanna
homeschool their kids

01:20:00.736 --> 01:20:03.186
without interference from the state,

01:20:03.186 --> 01:20:05.706
and together with them

01:20:05.706 --> 01:20:09.177
are these middle class
parents of trans kids.

01:20:09.177 --> 01:20:13.023
So the state will ultimately come after

01:20:13.023 --> 01:20:14.794
an awful lot of people.

01:20:14.794 --> 01:20:17.634
And perhaps there's some hope in that,

01:20:17.634 --> 01:20:22.134
that we can build meaningful
coalitions like that one,

01:20:23.015 --> 01:20:24.265
but also others

01:20:26.162 --> 01:20:28.745
to stop the taking of children.

01:20:35.003 --> 01:20:38.184
- [Audience] Hey, I was
really curious to know

01:20:38.184 --> 01:20:40.767
about child taking in Honduras.

01:20:42.964 --> 01:20:44.322
- Child taking in?

01:20:44.322 --> 01:20:45.913
- [Audience] Honduras,

01:20:45.913 --> 01:20:50.752
and also if you could talk
a little bit about why

01:20:50.752 --> 01:20:55.669
in El Salvador there were more
cases of recovering children

01:20:56.913 --> 01:21:00.083
than in some of the other countries.

01:21:00.083 --> 01:21:00.916
If you could just talk

01:21:00.916 --> 01:21:02.933
a little bit more about why that could be?

01:21:02.933 --> 01:21:04.266
- Civil society.

01:21:06.762 --> 01:21:10.741
The ability to build
the civil society group

01:21:10.741 --> 01:21:14.241
that could reach across the whole country.

01:21:15.243 --> 01:21:19.993
And everybody in El Salvador
more or less speaks Spanish.

01:21:22.191 --> 01:21:23.774
Also, the fact that

01:21:29.523 --> 01:21:31.910
I'm trying to think of a
word that does some justice.

01:21:31.910 --> 01:21:34.827
Very conservative military members,

01:21:36.243 --> 01:21:38.826
and members of the dictatorship

01:21:40.980 --> 01:21:43.397
stayed in power in Guatemala.

01:21:50.799 --> 01:21:55.481
It was extremely difficult to
build anything in Guatemala

01:21:55.481 --> 01:21:58.320
that couldn't immediately be torn apart.

01:21:58.320 --> 01:22:03.237
I also think that the depth of
people's fears of each other

01:22:04.949 --> 01:22:09.449
in Guatemala is, especially,
as Indigenous communities

01:22:10.629 --> 01:22:13.129
were pitted against each other

01:22:14.171 --> 01:22:17.302
remains a really salient thing.

01:22:17.302 --> 01:22:19.518
The other thing is human rights activists

01:22:19.518 --> 01:22:21.968
are still getting killed by the police,

01:22:21.968 --> 01:22:24.103
and the military in Guatemala.

01:22:24.103 --> 01:22:25.370
If you wanna do this work,

01:22:25.370 --> 01:22:28.370
you're facing imprisonment or death.

01:22:30.208 --> 01:22:32.466
I started doing research in Guatemala

01:22:32.466 --> 01:22:37.096
about the time that
somebody in gender studies

01:22:37.096 --> 01:22:39.569
at the University of Central America

01:22:39.569 --> 01:22:42.648
got disappeared for doing this research.

01:22:42.648 --> 01:22:44.565
And I was like, oh, oh.

01:22:48.825 --> 01:22:49.742
Hoping that

01:22:51.246 --> 01:22:56.047
having the protections of
sort of a U.S. embassy,

01:22:56.047 --> 01:23:00.464
which we knew was complicit,
but nevertheless, having

01:23:01.975 --> 01:23:04.936
the thing about being white,

01:23:04.936 --> 01:23:08.508
and U.S. American gave me some protection,

01:23:08.508 --> 01:23:12.591
but it didn't for everybody,
so it's complicated.

01:23:22.525 --> 01:23:23.864
- [Audience] Laura, thank you so much.

01:23:23.864 --> 01:23:27.526
You've given me so much to think about.

01:23:27.526 --> 01:23:28.359
I wanna think with you,

01:23:28.359 --> 01:23:31.004
and just ask you to talk a little bit more

01:23:31.004 --> 01:23:34.066
about the status of the child

01:23:34.066 --> 01:23:37.483
in these different forms of child taking.

01:23:42.222 --> 01:23:45.472
I'm struck that in that narrative about

01:23:48.416 --> 01:23:50.584
making sure that when you take the child,

01:23:50.584 --> 01:23:53.635
you are canceling the future, right?

01:23:53.635 --> 01:23:55.302
And seems to me that

01:23:58.001 --> 01:24:01.013
what is also the present right now

01:24:01.013 --> 01:24:05.013
is we're also living
through all of these other,

01:24:06.203 --> 01:24:10.548
you not only have these cases
of actual taking, right?

01:24:10.548 --> 01:24:14.033
And the loss, but that
the law has made it,

01:24:14.033 --> 01:24:16.950
and the exercise of law enforcement

01:24:18.510 --> 01:24:22.343
has also been a way of
holding us in a process

01:24:23.435 --> 01:24:25.923
of a lot of other kinds of child taking,

01:24:25.923 --> 01:24:28.314
or child taking and potential.

01:24:28.314 --> 01:24:31.983
I was thinking about the TPS debates

01:24:31.983 --> 01:24:34.232
that are happening right now,

01:24:34.232 --> 01:24:37.732
and how that is a slowly unfurling process

01:24:38.670 --> 01:24:40.420
of family separation.

01:24:42.480 --> 01:24:44.469
I was on a call this morning

01:24:44.469 --> 01:24:48.668
'cause I'm giving testimony
for an asylum case

01:24:48.668 --> 01:24:51.335
for an Honduran family, a woman.

01:24:52.839 --> 01:24:55.288
And she will likely be deported

01:24:55.288 --> 01:24:57.788
because her four kids now have

01:24:59.839 --> 01:25:02.672
individually gotten asylum papers.

01:25:04.666 --> 01:25:07.348
And so the judge has decided

01:25:07.348 --> 01:25:12.177
that because those kids
are safe, ostensibly safe,

01:25:12.177 --> 01:25:16.288
they're gonna go into foster
care she can be deported.

01:25:16.288 --> 01:25:20.205
And so in some cases of
what you are looking at

01:25:21.226 --> 01:25:24.726
the child is the direct target of violence

01:25:26.285 --> 01:25:27.846
just they themselves,

01:25:27.846 --> 01:25:29.596
like when we put kids

01:25:31.953 --> 01:25:34.235
in lockup with adults,

01:25:34.235 --> 01:25:37.494
and say they're MS-13 and they're animals.

01:25:37.494 --> 01:25:38.867
It doesn't matter that they're 11.

01:25:38.867 --> 01:25:41.284
It doesn't matter they're 13.

01:25:42.670 --> 01:25:47.420
Sometimes it's to hold them,
to hold the parents hostage,

01:25:50.056 --> 01:25:52.198
but in so many of these cases,

01:25:52.198 --> 01:25:56.397
the cases of genocide and the
killing of political enemies,

01:25:56.397 --> 01:25:58.697
I just wanna ask you just
sort of how you think about

01:25:58.697 --> 01:26:01.237
the different statuses of the child

01:26:01.237 --> 01:26:03.935
in those different acts of child taking

01:26:03.935 --> 01:26:06.087
'cause one of the things
that your study shows us

01:26:06.087 --> 01:26:10.337
is just how extraordinarily
flexible this form of terror is,

01:26:10.337 --> 01:26:14.838
and how just it weaves
itself through the society,

01:26:14.838 --> 01:26:17.798
and then the cartels
reproduce the child taking

01:26:17.798 --> 01:26:19.367
in their own operations.

01:26:19.367 --> 01:26:21.786
I'm just really inviting you to say more

01:26:21.786 --> 01:26:23.975
'cause it's so powerful
what you've been sharing.

01:26:23.975 --> 01:26:24.808
Thank you.

01:26:28.367 --> 01:26:32.039
- I know one of the things
I've thought a lot about is,

01:26:32.039 --> 01:26:34.066
or maybe not enough about,

01:26:34.066 --> 01:26:37.588
is that I'm actually much
better at thinking about

01:26:37.588 --> 01:26:42.148
the ways that child separation
is about the adults.

01:26:42.148 --> 01:26:44.148
And I think in that I am

01:26:46.798 --> 01:26:51.131
following so many of the
policymakers and ideologues

01:26:53.517 --> 01:26:57.600
who are producing the
rationale for child taking,

01:26:59.625 --> 01:27:03.208
but at the same time
it's really clear that

01:27:06.387 --> 01:27:08.776
it's not like once they get the child,

01:27:08.776 --> 01:27:13.257
then they imagine a humane
future for the child, right?

01:27:13.257 --> 01:27:15.590
On the contrary they imagine

01:27:18.067 --> 01:27:21.358
that gay kids will go
through conversion therapy,

01:27:21.358 --> 01:27:24.358
that trans kids will not transition.

01:27:25.558 --> 01:27:29.597
They imagine that Spanish speaking kids

01:27:29.597 --> 01:27:34.514
will be placed with Anglo
families who don't speak Spanish.

01:27:37.810 --> 01:27:39.776
There were some people commenting

01:27:39.776 --> 01:27:41.349
from the Trump administration.

01:27:41.349 --> 01:27:42.829
I was really trying to figure out,

01:27:42.829 --> 01:27:45.688
like, what were they thinking?

01:27:45.688 --> 01:27:48.771
And so to some extent they were like,

01:27:50.050 --> 01:27:54.633
I don't know, I guess we just
thought they'd go to DHS,

01:27:56.945 --> 01:27:58.887
and they'd disappear.

01:27:58.887 --> 01:28:02.887
And, like, there was no
way to imagine children.

01:28:04.928 --> 01:28:09.595
And I always sort of like
wanna vomit when I think about

01:28:11.986 --> 01:28:15.336
Scalia and conservatives claiming

01:28:15.336 --> 01:28:18.145
that children in immigration detention,

01:28:18.145 --> 01:28:21.886
there was so much visceral testimony

01:28:21.886 --> 01:28:23.855
that I didn't wanna quote, right?

01:28:23.855 --> 01:28:25.936
It's like, it's horrible,

01:28:25.936 --> 01:28:29.686
and I don't wanna sort
of produce the trauma,

01:28:32.497 --> 01:28:35.507
but that they then turned around and said,

01:28:35.507 --> 01:28:37.118
"Well, of course it's
fine to detain children

01:28:37.118 --> 01:28:42.118
as long as conditions are
humane, and obviously they are."

01:28:42.348 --> 01:28:43.431
And like how?

01:28:44.883 --> 01:28:48.050
Like how do you even sort of say that?

01:28:52.037 --> 01:28:52.870
So

01:28:54.858 --> 01:28:57.347
part of what's always fascinated me

01:28:57.347 --> 01:28:59.764
is the vanishing of children,

01:29:00.629 --> 01:29:04.879
like the imagination that
they will be produced as,

01:29:07.949 --> 01:29:12.199
I don't know, little white
Americans, or something.

01:29:17.637 --> 01:29:20.720
The story you tell from this morning,

01:29:21.937 --> 01:29:25.909
I wish I could say it surprises me at all.

01:29:25.909 --> 01:29:30.159
There was a moment, of course,
as you know, we all know,

01:29:30.159 --> 01:29:31.826
when having children

01:29:33.758 --> 01:29:35.425
was a reason to stay

01:29:36.718 --> 01:29:40.198
because it was cruel to
children to leave them,

01:29:40.198 --> 01:29:43.748
to separate them from their parents.

01:29:43.748 --> 01:29:45.240
- [Audience] Or their civil rights.

01:29:45.240 --> 01:29:46.073
- Right.

01:29:51.509 --> 01:29:54.926
And it's been now decades and decades of,

01:29:57.831 --> 01:30:00.809
you can do anything you want to parents

01:30:00.809 --> 01:30:03.892
if you can produce any account at all

01:30:05.230 --> 01:30:08.560
in which the children are sort of okay.

01:30:08.560 --> 01:30:12.246
It used to make me wanna
bang my head against the wall

01:30:12.246 --> 01:30:15.328
when I'd hear conservatives
start talking about

01:30:15.328 --> 01:30:17.480
anchor babies at precisely the moment

01:30:17.480 --> 01:30:20.601
when there was no such
thing anymore, right?

01:30:20.601 --> 01:30:23.601
It's like, how does this, so anyway,

01:30:24.482 --> 01:30:25.881
you've reached the level

01:30:25.881 --> 01:30:28.609
at which I'm completely
incoherent and speechless.

01:30:28.609 --> 01:30:30.276
I don't know how to.

01:30:32.808 --> 01:30:33.641
- [Stephen] Laura, on the contrary,

01:30:33.641 --> 01:30:34.812
you've been incredibly coherent,

01:30:34.812 --> 01:30:37.395
and given us a lot to dwell on.

01:30:38.584 --> 01:30:40.442
And I wanna thank you for your book,

01:30:40.442 --> 01:30:43.493
which I know many people have read,

01:30:43.493 --> 01:30:45.260
and would urge everyone to read.

01:30:45.260 --> 01:30:46.871
And thank you for being here,

01:30:46.871 --> 01:30:49.292
and I know you'll be seeing
some people tomorrow as well

01:30:49.292 --> 01:30:50.389
during your stay on campus,

01:30:50.389 --> 01:30:52.819
so we're grateful for that as well.

01:30:52.819 --> 01:30:55.991
And thank you to everybody
who made this event possible.

01:30:55.991 --> 01:30:57.520
Please join me in thanking Laura Briggs.

01:30:57.520 --> 01:31:00.687
(audience applauding)

01:31:03.739 --> 01:31:06.322
(bright music)

